window.GUIDES = [{"date":"2026-07-30","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"Lab kickoff — the AI Automation Lab opens","md":"# Lab kickoff — the AI Automation Lab opens\n\n*Thu Jul 30, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the day's log entry, the git history (78bb653 → fd25427) and DECISIONS.md — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nThe day the dropshipping business was closed and the AI automation business was opened in its place. Nothing was sold and nothing was built for a client. What got built was the **workshop**: a place to track the 30-day sprint, a place to learn from, and a memory that survives between sessions.\n\nThink of it as clearing the garage and hanging the tool board before the first job comes in.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters\n\n**ship.html — the sprint tracker.** A 30-day plan broken into 127 tasks, with tabs for Learn (the reading library), Build, Pitch, Pipeline and Money. Every task carries a decision note underneath it, so a future session can see *why* a box was ticked, not just that it was.\n\n**D001 — the first logged decision.** Sell to three blue-collar verticals only: Renovation / Landscaping / Roofing as the anchor, HVAC / Plumbing / Electrical second, Auto repair / Detailing third. No fourth vertical before Day 27. This is the rule that stopped every later \"what about dentists?\" detour.\n\n*Sales line that grew out of it:* \"I only work with trades. I know what a missed call costs you because that is all I look at.\"\n\n**CONTEXT.md + DECISIONS.md — persistent memory.** Two plain files in the repo. One says what is true right now, the other says what was decided and when. Every later session starts by reading them. This is why the project could run for six weeks across dozens of chats without losing the thread.\n\n**5 Core cleaned up.** The Master To-Do (NightSeal + Groomo) and the Reflecting / Generating off-day modes were retired from the personal app — the old business was actually gone, not just paused.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **Sprint** — a fixed block of days with a fixed list of tasks, so \"done\" has a definition.\n- **Decision log** — a numbered list (D001, D002…) of choices with the reason. You never re-argue a numbered decision; you either follow it or write a new one that supersedes it.\n- **Vertical** — one type of business (plumbers, roofers). Picking few of them is what lets the pitch sound specific.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n- Open `ship.html` on the site — the 127 tasks and their decision notes are still there.\n- In the repo, open `DECISIONS.md` and find **D001**. If a later decision changed the verticals, it is numbered below it.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **Write the memory before the work.** CONTEXT.md and DECISIONS.md cost an hour on day one and saved days later.\n- **Limit the market before touching a tool.** D001 is a constraint, and constraints are what made the later machines specific enough to demo.\n\n## Sources\ngit 78bb653…fd25427 · DECISIONS.md D001 · journal entry 2026-07-30\n"},{"date":"2026-08-02","group":"work","kind":"cheatsheet","label":"Day 2 cheat sheet","origin":"original","source":"SecondRing Vault/Day 2 Cheat Sheet.md","title":"Day 2 cheat sheet — webhooks, APIs, JSON","md":"# Day 2 cheat sheet — webhooks, APIs, JSON\n*Filled from live runs on 2026-08-02. Re-read before every client call.*\n\n## The mental model\n**A webhook is a doorbell with a note attached.** Something happens (missed call, form submit) → a note (the data) gets delivered to an address (the URL). The whole business is: read the note, do something useful, automatically.\n\n## The one-liners\n- **Webhook** — \"when X happens, POST the details to this URL\"\n- **API** — the door software opens so other software can ask it things\n- **Endpoint** — one specific door (one URL that accepts one kind of request)\n- **JSON** — the note written neatly: `{\"name\":\"Dave\",\"urgency\":\"today\"}`\n- **Payload** — whatever the note says (the body of the request)\n- **Header** — the envelope's outside: what format it's in, who sent it, proof it's real\n- **Auth token** — the password one piece of software shows another\n\n## What I saw happen (live, today)\n1. webhook.site gave a unique URL. Two POSTs landed instantly:\n   - Twilio-style, form-encoded: `CallSid=...&From=%2B1519...&DialCallStatus=no-answer` — ugly `key=value&` pairs. `%2B` is an encoded `+`.\n   - JSON lead: `{\"name\":\"Dave M\",\"service\":\"furnace not heating\",\"urgency\":\"today\"}` — clean and nested.\n   - **Same mechanism, two dialects. Twilio speaks form-encoded; most web things speak JSON; n8n reads both.**\n2. `DialCallStatus` is the single field Build 1 branches on. `From` is who gets texted back.\n3. Google Form → linked Sheet → email notification: submitted \"Dave Miller / furnace not heating\" → row appeared at 12:58:39, email fired, zero human touches.\n\n## The breaks (watched them happen)\n- **Renamed \"Phone number\" → \"customer_cell\", submitted again.** Forms still wrote correctly (it tracks columns internally) — BUT anything external reading columns *by name* (n8n) would now fail **silently**. No error. Just missing data, found weeks later. Clients \"tidying\" spreadsheets is the #1 automation killer.\n- **Submitted \"José Müller 😀\" / \"519 555 12 34 ext.7\" / \"fence??? maybe just a quote idk\".** Landed intact — but try texting that phone number. Real data is always messy; builds must clean, not hope.\n\n## The law\n**Automations are contracts — exact names, exact formats. Humans break contracts constantly. Build for the mess.**\n\n## Client-call translations (never say the jargon)\n- Webhook → \"the instant a call is missed, the system gets a ping\"\n- API → \"the door into your calendar/CRM that lets these tools talk\"\n- Payload → \"the details that ride along — who called, when, from what number\"\n- Silent failure → \"it doesn't crash, it just quietly stops catching leads — that's why I monitor it\"\n\n## Rep log (updated)\n1. ✅ Clean build: Form → Sheet → email, zero-touch\n2. ✅ Column rename → silent contract drift (still drifting — `customer_cell` accepts data fine, any name-based reader would be broken)\n3. ✅ Garbage data: José Müller 😀 / \"519 555 12 34 ext.7\" — landed intact, builds must clean it\n4. ✅ Race: two POSTs same second (14:18:53) — both captured. Sheet writes queue fine; POLLING triggers are where races drop rows. Webhooks (push) don't. This is why Build 1 is webhook-based.\n5. ✅ Credential failure (a REAL one, better than the planned rep): Google Sheets credential returned **403 Forbidden** — cause was *identity*, not the password. Credential = ahmed@secondring.ca, sheet owned by the personal Gmail. Fix: share the sheet across accounts. **Multi-account confusion is the #1 \"but the credential is green!\" failure — check WHO the credential is, not just THAT it connects.**\n\n**Bonus rep (real bug, n8n):** Switch dropped all items silently — field was in *Fixed* mode comparing literal `{{ }}` text. Fix: Expression toggle, confirm with `fx` + green preview.\n**Meta-lesson:** a form is just a webhook wearing a shirt — extracted `entry.NNN` IDs from the form HTML and fired raw POSTs at `formResponse`. Same mechanism as Twilio → n8n. It's all POSTs.\n\n## Still to do with my own hands\n- [ ] Connect Google Sheets credential in n8n (2-click OAuth) → then run rep 5\n- [ ] The thing I'll forget in 3 weeks: the rename broke *nothing visible*. That's what makes it dangerous.\n"},{"date":"2026-08-02","group":"work","kind":"log","label":"Session log","origin":"original","source":"SecondRing Vault/Session Log 2026-08-02.md","title":"Session Log — Sunday, Aug 2, 2026 (Day 1 of sprint)","md":"# Session Log — Sunday, Aug 2, 2026 (Day 1 of sprint)\n\n## THE ONE BLOCKER FOR NEXT SESSION\n**Upgrade Twilio (~$20 first credit load).** Everything in Build 1 works; the trial refuses custom-worded SMS (policy, not a bug). After upgrading: re-fire the test → phone buzzes → record it (Build 1 Loom opener). Upgrade also lifts verified-numbers-only and lets you buy a local 226 number to replace the Texas trial number.\n\n---\n\n## Business identity\n- **Company/domain:** secondring.ca (Namecheap, expires Jun 16 2027)\n- **Business email:** ahmed@secondring.ca — Google Workspace, verified, Gmail LIVE\n- **Personal email:** the personal Gmail (Chrome is signed into this one)\n- ⚠️ **Two-identity rule:** browser/Forms/Sheets = blonded gmail · n8n Google credential = ahmed@secondring. Anything n8n must touch has to be shared to ahmed@secondring.ca.\n- **Cell (verified in Twilio):** +1 437 ••• ••••\n- **Territory decision:** Waterloo Region (Kitchener·Waterloo·Cambridge·Guelph), NOT GTA — logged\n\n## DNS state (all verified live)\n- MX: `1 smtp.google.com` (Custom MX, TTL 1 min)\n- SPF: `v=spf1 include:_spf.google.com ~all`\n- DKIM: `google._domainkey` record exists — **still verify it's switched ON in Admin → Gmail → Authenticate email**\n- DMARC: `_dmarc` → `v=DMARC1; p=none; rua=mailto:dmarc@secondring.ca; pct=100`\n  - **TODO:** create `dmarc@secondring.ca` alias/group or reports bounce\n  - **TODO:** after 2–4 weeks of reports, tighten p=none → quarantine\n- Leftover: `@` URL-redirect → www → Namecheap parking page (harmless; replace when real site exists)\n\n## Accounts\n- **Twilio:** trial, 27 days left · trial number **+1 737 221 2163** (US)\n- **n8n Cloud:** ahmeddakhil.app.n8n.cloud · 14-day trial · credentials: \"Twilio account\" ✓, \"Google Sheets account\" (ahmed@secondring.ca) ✓\n- 8 sprint accounts created per user (n8n, Make, Airtable, Cal.com, Twilio, Google Cloud, Anthropic)\n\n## Build 1 — Missed-Call Rescue (workflow: /workflow/ZeESw6UxZhdmfQCK)\nWebhook (POST, path a9df9c7d-…8197bc) → Switch on `{{ $json.body.DialCallStatus }}`\n- rule \"no-answer\" → output **missed** → [Twilio SMS node — built, gated by trial] + [Sheets Append — WORKING]\n- rule \"completed\" → output **answered** (no-op)\n- SMS: From +17372212163 · To `{{ $json.body.From }}` · msg \"Sorry we missed your call - this is Ahmed at SecondRing…\"\n- **Log sheet:** \"Build1 Log\" (id 1Qq9LaRD3_xMjB…cDIw), owned by blonded, shared to ahmed@secondring — DON'T unshare. Columns: caller/status/time. Test row landed: `1437••••••• | no-answer | 14:37:38`.\n- Full spec: `build-1-missed-call-rescue-spec.md` (outputs folder — copy into vault)\n\n## Sprint scoreboard\n- **Day 1: 5/5 DONE** (verticals, accounts, domain+mailbox, vault, Pitch tab read)\n- **Day 2: DONE** — webhook fired at webhook.site AND n8n; 5/5 reps (3 were real bugs); cheat sheet written from live evidence\n- **Day 3: ~60%** — spec complete + skeleton built/tested; remains: business-hours node, follow-up sweeper, 10 tests, Loom (post-upgrade)\n- **Day 15: DONE on Day 1** — 100 scored prospects (30 verified + 70 screened, files on Desktop)\n- **Day 16: head start** — cold-open script + phone-stack questions written (call-audit-playbook.md)\n- **TODO: tick all boxes in ship.html tracker** (saves as you type)\n\n## Hard-won lessons (all in Day 2 Cheat Sheet)\n1. **Fixed vs Expression toggle** — field shows `{{ }}` but compares literal text; no error, silently matches nothing. Check `fx` + green preview.\n2. **403 with a green credential = wrong identity**, not wrong password (ahmed@ vs blonded@).\n3. Rename a column → nothing visibly breaks → name-based readers die silently weeks later.\n4. Forms = webhooks wearing shirts; polling drops racing rows, webhooks don't.\n5. Twilio: auth error ≠ params error — the error text names the broken layer. Read it.\n\n## Assets on disk\n- Desktop/SecondRing Vault/ — 44 pages (Home, 8 builds, 30 prospects + bench, templates, cheat sheet, this log)\n- Desktop/call-audit-lead-list-31-100.csv + call-audit-excluded-batch-2.csv\n- Outputs folder (session): lead list 1-30, bench/excluded, call log CSV, playbook, Build 1 spec\n- Live: Service Request demo form + response sheet (keep the messy José row — teaching prop), webhook.site inbox (7-day expiry)\n\n## Call-audit facts worth repeating\n- Cambridge/Guelph plumber listings are ~half lead-gen shells (\"[Name] Plumbers of [City]\" pattern, 290 King St E + 500 Fairway Rd S virtual offices, 289/647/866 numbers)\n- Best prospects rate 4.9–5.0 with no website — competence + overflow, not failure\n- Audit stat for the Loom: called 30 shops → most rang out → none called back same day (fill real numbers after silent-audit round: weekday 4:15–5:00 p.m.; conversations 7–8 a.m.)\n"},{"date":"2026-08-02","group":"work","kind":"build","label":"Build 1 page","origin":"original","source":"SecondRing Vault/Builds/Build 1 - Missed-Call Rescue.md","title":"BUILD 1 — Missed-Call Rescue","md":"---\nbuild_no: 1\nstatus: in-progress\nhours_budgeted: 6\ndemo_loom:\nworkflow: https://ahmeddakhil.app.n8n.cloud/workflow/ZeESw6UxZhdmfQCK\n---\n\n> **✅ LOGGING LIVE (2026-08-02 pm):** Sheets branch added and tested — missed call → row in \"Build1 Log\" (`1437••••••• | no-answer | 14:37:38`). Mappings: caller=`{{ $json.body.From }}`, status=`{{ $json.body.DialCallStatus }}`, time=`{{ $now }}`. Sheet is owned by the personal Gmail, shared to ahmed@secondring.ca (the n8n credential identity) — don't unshare it.\n>\n> **⚠️ BLOCKER (2026-08-02):** Everything works — webhook ✓ routing ✓ credential ✓ To-expression resolves to caller ✓. Twilio **trial** rejects custom SMS bodies (\"trial accounts can only use predefined templates\"). **Fix = upgrade Twilio (~$20 credit).** No build changes needed after. Upgrade also removes verified-numbers-only limit + enables buying a local 226 number to replace +1 737 221 2163.\n>\n> **2026-08-02:** Skeleton live in n8n — Webhook (POST) → Switch on `{{ $json.body.DialCallStatus }}` with `missed` / `answered` outputs. Tested with a fake Twilio payload: routes correctly to `missed`.\n> **Bug found & fixed:** rule value was in *Fixed* mode, comparing the literal text `{{ ... }}` to \"no-answer\" — silently matched nothing. Toggle to **Expression** (the `fx` icon + green Result preview confirms). Check this FIRST whenever a Switch/IF drops items.\n\n# BUILD 1 — Missed-Call Rescue\n\n**One-sentence pitch:** You don't answer, so they call the next guy — this texts them back before they do.\n\n> Full spec written 2026-08-02 (architecture, TwiML, n8n workflows, sweeper pattern, 10 tests, Loom script, CASL notes). Lives in the sprint outputs folder as `build-1-missed-call-rescue-spec.md` — copy it into this vault next to this page.\n\n## Architecture\nTwilio number (forwards to owner's real cell, 20s timeout) → DialCallStatus webhook → n8n → business-hours check → open/after-hours SMS variant → Google Sheet log → sweeper follow-ups (+10 min, +1 day, +3 days) → inbound reply kills sequence + alerts owner.\n\n**Key design point:** owner keeps their number; Twilio forwards to it. This kills the \"I don't want a new number\" objection.\n\n## Build log\n- What broke:\n- What I'd do differently:\n- Time actual vs budgeted:\n\n## Tests (the 10 that matter)\n- [ ] Missed call in hours → open template\n- [ ] Missed call 11 p.m. → after-hours template\n- [ ] Answered call → NO SMS (most common bug)\n- [ ] Hangup before ring-out → no SMS\n- [ ] Busy → SMS fires\n- [ ] Immediate reply → follow-ups stop, owner alerted\n- [ ] Reply after touch 2 → touches 3–4 suppressed\n- [ ] No reply → all touches at correct intervals\n- [ ] Same number twice in an hour → dedupe, no double-text\n- [ ] STOP → opt-out honoured everywhere\n\n## Demo\n- [ ] 90-second Loom — open with the audit stat: \"I called 30 trades shops around Waterloo Region...\"\n- Link:\n\n## Client version\n- Setup time: ~20 min on their line\n- Twilio cost to client: ~$10–15/mo at 100 missed calls (verify current rates)\n- Price frame: their arithmetic first — misses/week × job value × 50. Never quote before they say their number.\n"},{"date":"2026-08-02","group":"work","kind":"hq","label":"HQ page","origin":"original","source":"SecondRing Vault/Home.md","title":"SecondRing — HQ","md":"# SecondRing — HQ\n\n> **⚠️ NEXT SESSION — ONE BLOCKER:** Build 1 is fully wired and tested; the ONLY thing stopping the live SMS is the **Twilio trial** — it refuses custom-worded texts (policy, not a bug). **Upgrade Twilio (~$20 first credit load)** → the workflow fires as-is, zero changes. Upgrading also lifts \"verified numbers only\" and lets you swap the Texas trial number (+1 737 221 2163) for a local 226. Needed before any real shop demo. Then: re-fire the test, phone buzzes, record it — that's the Build 1 Loom opener.\n\n**AI Automation Lab · Waterloo Region** (switched from GTA — decision logged 2026-08-02)\nDomain: secondring.ca · verified, Gmail live, SPF/DKIM/DMARC configured\n\n## The bet\n30 days, 160 hours → 11 portfolio assets, a rehearsed pitch, 400+ businesses contacted, 2 paying clients.\n\n## Structure\n- **Builds/** — one page per build. The demo Loom link, what broke, what the client version needs. This is your case-study source.\n- **Prospects/** — one page per prospect, pre-filled with audit data. Update after every call. Never rely on memory.\n- **Templates/** — copy these when adding new builds or prospects.\n\n## Verticals (pick 3, no 4th)\nThe lead list spans 8 trades. Sharpest gaps found in the audit:\n1. **Auto repair** — best no-website density ([[01 - Prestige Auto Repair]], [[02 - C S Automotive]])\n2. **Tree service** — 24h claims nobody backs, family ops ([[03 - A-1 Tree Service]])\n3. **Plumbing · HVAC · garage door** — emergency after-hours calls, highest-value misses\n\n## Operating rules\n- Log the call before dialing the next one\n- Never claim to be a customer\n- Say \"the phone\", never \"AI\"\n- End the call at 60 seconds. Every time.\n\n## Day 1 checklist — COMPLETE ✅\n- [x] 3 verticals chosen\n- [x] 8 free accounts (n8n Cloud, Make, Airtable, Cal.com, Twilio, Google Cloud, Anthropic Console)\n- [x] Domain + mailbox (secondring.ca — verified 2026-08-02, SPF/DKIM/DMARC configured)\n- [x] Vault set up\n- [x] Read the Pitch tab end to end\n\n**Day 2 also complete. Full state → [[Session Log 2026-08-02]]**\n"},{"date":"2026-08-04","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"Sprint Days 2–5 — roadmap page shipped, Builds 1–3 banked","md":"# Sprint Days 2–5 — roadmap page shipped, Builds 1–3 banked\n\n*Around Tue Aug 4, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry, the git history (144d2bb, 5c2585a) and the sprint tracker state — not written on the day. Day 2's own cheat sheet (webhooks, APIs, JSON) is attached to Aug 2.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nThe first stretch of real building. By the end of it the three machines that every later pitch stands on existed and had been tested, and the site gained the roadmap page that the whole sprint is steered from.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters on a sales call\n\n**Build 1 — Missed-Call Rescue.** A call rings out → the caller gets a text within seconds → the text is checked against STOP (opt-out) → an AI reads the reply and grades it → the owner gets an alert only when it matters. *Sales line:* \"You don't answer, so they call the next guy. This texts them back before they do.\"\n\n**Build 2 — Speed-to-Lead Responder.** A web-form lead gets a reply in about three seconds, and the lead is graded hot / warm / junk before the owner ever sees it. *Sales line:* \"The first shop to answer usually gets the job. This makes you first, every time, even at 11pm.\"\n\n**Build 3 — Review Engine.** After a job the customer is asked how it went. A 4 or 5 gets the Google review link; a 1 to 3 is intercepted and goes privately to the owner. *Sales line:* \"Happy customers get pointed at Google. Unhappy ones get pointed at you, first.\"\n\n**roadmap.html.** A progress ring, a count of remaining blocks, a library of the construction builds, and a link both ways between 5 Core and the roadmap. From here on the roadmap card is the plan; a stale card sends you down the old path.\n\n**Sprint tracker.** Days 1–6 and 8 ticked: APIs / webhooks / JSON, the three builds, error handling + security, and self-hosting n8n.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **n8n** — the workflow tool. Boxes on a canvas: \"when this webhook fires → check this → send that\". It is the nervous system of every machine.\n- **Twilio** — the phone company for software. It owns the number, forwards the call, sends the text.\n- **Triage / grading** — the AI reading a message and sorting it (hot, warm, junk) so the owner only looks at what matters.\n- **STOP check** — before any text goes out, ask \"did this person opt out?\" It is the one rule that beats every other rule.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n- Open `roadmap.html` → the build library lists Builds 1–3 with what each does.\n- The sprint tracker ticks live in your browser under `lab.sprint.done.v1` (Settings → Application → Local storage in Chrome) — that is where \"Days 1–6 and 8 done\" is stored.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **Three machines, told as three sentences.** Every later demo script is these three sales lines with numbers filled in.\n- **The roadmap page is part of the build.** Changing the plan without changing the card is how weeks get lost.\n\n## Sources\ngit 144d2bb, 5c2585a · lab.sprint.done.v1 · journal entry 2026-08-04\n"},{"date":"2026-08-07","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"Roadmap v2 — platform first, 37 machines, the showroom model","md":"# Roadmap v2 — platform first, 37 machines, the showroom model\n\n*Fri Aug 7, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry and git 40e3d3b — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nThe day the business stopped being \"a few automations\" and became \"an operating system for trade businesses\". Five machines were banked, the server that runs them was hardened, and the roadmap was rewritten so that the **platform** gets built before any more machines do.\n\nThe analogy: five good tools in a pickup is a handyman. A shop with a panel, a schedule and a service log is a company. Today was deciding to build the shop.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters\n\n**Five machines banked.** Builds 1–3 (Missed-Call Rescue, Speed-to-Lead, Review Engine) plus the **Missed-Appointment Log** and the **Estimate Follow-up Ladder** (a quote that has gone quiet gets a polite nudge sequence instead of being forgotten).\n\n**Infrastructure standing.** A $6/month Toronto droplet (a small rented Linux server) at `auto.secondring.ca` with HTTPS; Twilio repointed at it; nightly 3am backups verified; the encryption key vaulted; the error watchdog tested by deliberately breaking things (failure injection); 3-retry on 26 nodes; keys rotated; an onboarding playbook written.\n\n*Why a client cares:* \"It runs on my own server in Toronto, it backs itself up every night, and if something breaks I get told before you do.\"\n\n**The new plan, in order.** Supabase (one shared database) → the control layer (modes, gates, logging) → a Lovable showroom (the owner's dashboard) → migrate the five builds onto it → then the remaining machines to reach **37** → then the pitch. Platform first, because every machine built before the platform had to be rebuilt once (Build 3 was, on Aug 8).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **Droplet** — DigitalOcean's name for a rented virtual server. Ours is 1 CPU / 1 GB, Toronto.\n- **Supabase** — a hosted Postgres database with sign-in and row-level security built in. One database, many clients, each sees only its own rows.\n- **Lovable** — a tool that builds web dashboards from a description. It draws the Owner Console; it does not run the machines.\n- **Showroom** — the demo site where a prospect sees the machines running on a modelled company before buying.\n- **Failure injection** — breaking something on purpose to prove the alarm goes off.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n- `https://auto.secondring.ca` answers over HTTPS — that is the droplet.\n- `roadmap.html` still carries the \"37 machines\" catalogue and the section order Supabase → control layer → showroom → migrate.\n- On the droplet, the nightly backup is the 3am cron job (`crontab -l`).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **Build the platform before the tenth tool, not after.** Everything built before Aug 7 was rebuilt at least once.\n- **Harden the server the same week you rent it.** Swap, backups, watchdog and key rotation are cheaper before the first client than after.\n\n## Sources\ngit 40e3d3b · journal entry 2026-08-07\n"},{"date":"2026-08-08","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"Platform — Build 3 rebuilt Sheets-free with five Control Gates","md":"# Platform — Build 3 rebuilt Sheets-free with five Control Gates\n\n*Sat Aug 8, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry and ledger §5.5 (Aug 8–9) — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nThe first machine moved off Google Sheets and onto the platform, and the first version of the **Control Gate** idea was written down: every message a machine sends has to pass a set of checks first. It was also the day a bad import doubled a workflow into an 82-node mess, which is the lesson most worth keeping.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters on a sales call\n\n**Build 3 (Review Engine) rebuilt without Sheets.** Its data now lives in the database instead of a spreadsheet a client can accidentally \"tidy\". Five Control Gates sit in front of the send.\n\n**The five gates (first version).** The record says \"5 Control Gates\" and does not list them one by one. What is certain is what they grew into: by Day 13 the checks were one shared database function every machine calls, in a fixed order — opt-out, off/paused, Watch, owner approval, taken-over, quiet hours, daily cap. Today they were nodes inside one workflow; the idea was the same.\n\n*Sales line:* \"Nothing goes out to your customer without passing the same checks — off switch, opt-out, quiet hours, daily limit, your approval.\"\n\n**The Master Service Architecture became the `ai-trade-business-os` skill** (Aug 8–9): six suites, 37 machines, the eight guardrails, packaging. From here the roadmap describes an *AI operating system for trade businesses*, not a list of automations.\n\n**The 82-node blowup.** n8n's *Import from file* **appends** to the open workflow instead of replacing it. One import doubled the workflow into an 82-node tangle. Restored from the backup commit `af7deec2`. Lesson banked; see below.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **Control Gate** — a checkpoint in front of a send. If any check fails, the message is held and the reason is logged.\n- **Quiet hours** — the window (evenings, early mornings) when no customer gets texted, emergencies excepted.\n- **Daily cap** — the maximum number of messages one person can get from one business in a day.\n- **Append vs replace** — adding on top of what is there vs swapping it out. Import-from-file does the first; you usually want the second.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n- In n8n, open the Review Engine workflow: there is no Google Sheets node left in it.\n- In the repo history, `af7deec2` is the backup the workflow was restored from.\n- The skill text (the \"Master Service Architecture\") is what every later session loads first.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **Before importing into n8n, open a blank workflow.** Import appends. Always.\n- **Commit the workflow JSON before touching it.** The restore took minutes because a backup existed.\n- **Gates belong in front of every send, not inside one machine.** Today they were five nodes in Build 3; by Day 13 they were one shared rule. Start the pattern early even if it moves later.\n\n## Sources\nledger §5.5 Aug 8 · journal entry 2026-08-08\n"},{"date":"2026-08-09","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"Platform — P1–P3 audited live, Build 5 migrated, SMS Router built","md":"# Platform — P1–P3 audited live, Build 5 migrated, SMS Router built\n\n*Sun Aug 9, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry, git e2679d6 / 1ad270f and ledger §5.5 — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nThe platform's first **audit day**. Instead of building more, the three foundation phases (P1 database, P2 control layer, P3 showroom) were tested live to see whether the promises held. They did. Then two machines were moved onto the platform and the inbound text traffic was given a single front door.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters on a sales call\n\n**RLS holds tenant to tenant.** Row-level security is the database rule that says \"a business can only ever read its own rows\". Tested live: one tenant cannot see another's leads, jobs or messages. *Sales line:* \"Your data is in the same database as other shops the way your money is in the same bank — nobody else can see your account.\"\n\n**The approvals inbox works end to end.** A machine proposes a message → it lands in the owner's approvals inbox in Lovable → the owner approves → only then does it send. This is the \"Ask me\" mode a client can run every machine in.\n\n**`events_append_only()` blocked a superuser delete.** The events table is the receipt for everything a machine did. A database trigger refuses deletes and edits — even from the admin account. Proved by trying. *Sales line:* \"The log cannot be edited, not even by me.\"\n\n**Build 3 cut over; Build 5 migrated.** Review Engine now runs on the platform. The Estimate Follow-up Ladder moved too.\n\n**SMS Router built.** One inbound webhook receives every text reply and decides where it goes (later: STOP → Review → Booking → triage). Before this, each machine had its own way in.\n\n**P4 marked 6/6; roadmap reorganised** around the AI Trade Business OS sections (e2679d6, 1ad270f).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **RLS (row-level security)** — a per-row lock. The database itself enforces who can see what, so a bug in a workflow cannot leak another client's data.\n- **Append-only** — you can add to the log, never change or remove. That is what makes it a receipt.\n- **Superuser** — the all-powerful admin login. If the trigger stops *that*, it stops everyone.\n- **Router** — one entry point that reads an incoming message and hands it to the right machine.\n- **Cut over** — the moment the live traffic starts using the new version instead of the old one.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\nIn the Supabase SQL editor:\n\n```sql\nselect count(*) from events;   -- the receipt keeps growing\nselect tgname from pg_trigger where tgname ilike '%append_only%';   -- the trigger is still there\n```\n\nPick any one `events` row and try to delete it — the trigger refuses, even as admin. That is the proof from Aug 9, still true today.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **Audit before you migrate.** Moving five machines onto a foundation that had not been tested would have doubled the work if it had failed.\n- **One front door for inbound.** The router is why STOP could later be enforced everywhere with one function.\n- **\"Blocked a superuser delete\" is the sentence that sells the log.** Keep proving it live.\n\n## Sources\ngit e2679d6, 1ad270f · ledger §5.5 Aug 9 · journal entry 2026-08-09\n"},{"date":"2026-08-11","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"Ops — memory files and the role split","md":"# Ops — memory files and the role split\n\n*Tue Aug 11, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry and ledger §5.5 — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nA short operations day. No machine changed. Two memory documents were written and the question \"which tool does which job?\" was settled for good, which stopped a lot of later thrashing.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters\n\n**MASTER-MEMORY.md and LOVABLE-MASTER-CONTEXT.md.** The first is the single file a new Claude session reads to know where the business stands. The second is the same thing for Lovable — what the database looks like, what the console must show, what the modes mean — so the dashboard tool never builds from a guess.\n\n**The role split.**\n- **Claude** = machine brains, backend, documents, the site. Anything that runs, writes to the database, or gets published.\n- **Lovable** = dashboards only. It draws the Owner Console from the context file; it does not run machines.\n- **ChatGPT** = brainstorming. Ideas, wording, second opinions; nothing it says is a decision until it is logged.\n\n*Why it matters on a sales call:* nothing, directly — but it is why the demo has one consistent story. Every tool reads the same memory, so the console, the machines and the pitch agree with each other.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **Context file** — a document written *for a tool*, not a person: the facts it needs before it starts.\n- **Source of truth** — the one place a fact is allowed to live. Memory files point at it; they do not replace it.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n- Both files live in the repo. The habit that came out of this day is still visible: every day's findings, startup prompt and study guide are written to the project at close.\n- The `ai-trade-business-os` skill (§5.6 \"how he works with Claude\") is the grown-up version of MASTER-MEMORY.md.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **A tool without a context file builds from a guess.** Lovable's early console problems were context problems.\n- **Assign the job to the tool, once.** After Aug 11, \"should Lovable do this?\" had a standing answer.\n\n## Sources\nledger §5.5 Aug 11 · journal entry 2026-08-11\n"},{"date":"2026-08-12","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"A · Day 1 — clean slate + machine catalogue","md":"# A · Day 1 — clean slate + machine catalogue\n\n*Wed Aug 12, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry, git f2a42c7 / b64e013 / 59a8c35 / 8395ca4 / aad7199 and ledger §5.5 — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nCategory A, Day 1 — the first day of the dated sprint. The roadmap was cut into 16 day-cards (66 boxes) that line up with your days off, Build 1 was moved onto the platform, and a week-long mystery alarm was traced to its real cause.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters on a sales call\n\n**The roadmap became 16 dated day-cards.** 66 boxes, synced to the 2-2-3 rotation, 5am–5pm on off days. The off-day schedule was pulled into the 5 Core calendar and day view, with a reflection slot in the 2–4pm block. From here on, \"what do I do today?\" has one answer in one place.\n\n**Build 1 migrated to Supabase `leads` and cut over live.** Missed-Call Rescue no longer writes to a Google Sheet; every missed call becomes a row in the shared database, with the same RLS protection as everything else. *Sales line:* \"Every missed call you have ever had is a row you can search — not a spreadsheet tab.\"\n\n**The watchdog storm, explained.** The error watchdog had been firing for days. Cause: the Google Sheets OAuth token. The Google Cloud app was still in *Testing* mode, and testing-mode tokens expire every **7 days** — so every week the Sheets credential silently died and every Sheets node failed. Moving off Sheets (above) is the permanent fix.\n\n**Grand River Mechanical v1 profile written** — the first realistic second-client profile, used later for tenant #2.\n\n**Honest note carried in the record:** Phase 0 SQL files 001–008 were never applied. \"Written\" and \"applied\" are different words; this is the first time the ledger says so out loud.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **OAuth token** — the temporary pass one app holds to act on your Google account. In a *Testing*-mode Google Cloud app it expires in 7 days; in a *Published* app it does not.\n- **Watchdog** — the script that watches n8n and texts when something fails. A storm is when it fires constantly for one root cause.\n- **Cut over** — the switch from the old path (Sheets) to the new path (database) for live traffic.\n- **Day-card** — one roadmap card per off day, four or five boxes each.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n- Open `roadmap.html` — the day-cards start at Aug 12 and carry these boxes: bA-0 … bA-4.\n- In Supabase: `select count(*) from leads;` — Build 1's rows live here now.\n- In Google Cloud Console → OAuth consent screen: if it says **Testing**, every token it issues still dies in 7 days.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **A weekly failure is a 7-day token until proven otherwise.**\n- **Sync the plan to real off days.** Day-cards dated to the rotation are the reason the sprint kept moving through 12-hour shifts.\n- **Say \"never applied\" in the record.** It is what let Day 15 find the rate-card DDL had not run either.\n\n## Sources\ngit f2a42c7, b64e013, 59a8c35, 8395ca4, aad7199 · ledger §5.5 Aug 12 · journal entry 2026-08-12\n"},{"date":"2026-08-13","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"A · Day 2 — the client-onboarding customization system","md":"# A · Day 2 — the client-onboarding customization system\n\n*Thu Aug 13 – Fri Aug 14, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry, git 1801b0b and ledger §5.5 — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nThe day the business got a **front door for new clients**. `onboarding.html` is a form that knows every machine and every setting a machine has; filling it in for a business writes that business into the database with no hand-written SQL. It is also the day the first standing rule was written: *a machine without a registry entry is not deployable*.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters on a sales call\n\n**`onboarding.html` shipped.** It is *registry-driven*: a `MACHINES` list in the page describes each machine and its settings (`config[]`), what it *requires* to work, and an owner-facing blurb. The form renders from that list, so adding a setting to the registry adds it to the form.\n\n- `sr_onboard_tenant(jsonb)` — the database function the form calls with the whole submission.\n- `ONBOARD_SECRET` — a shared secret so only the form can call it.\n- **Patch mode** — re-submit for an existing client to change one thing, not everything.\n- **Drift views** — database views that show where the form and the database disagree.\n\n**Schema v4 with the send-governance layer** — the tables that later hold modes, caps, quiet hours and approvals per tenant.\n\n*Sales line:* \"Setting you up is a form, not a project. Twenty minutes, and every machine knows your hours, your tone, your review link and who to call.\"\n\n**The `onboard-new-machine` skill saved** — the checklist that must run every time a machine is finished, renamed, gains a setting, or is demoed. Its test: *would two clients ever want a different value?* If yes, it is a registry setting, never hard-coded.\n\n**Meta.** Business verification needs a legal entity, which did not exist yet → decision: \"Messenger via Twilio BSP now, own app later.\" (The entity arrived Aug 20.)\n\n**Published as-is.** Internal docs, SQL and n8n JSON went public with the page. Known and accepted at the time.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **Registry** — the one list that says which machines exist and what each can be configured with. The form, the console and the runners all read it.\n- **Tenant** — one client business inside the shared database.\n- **`jsonb`** — a database column that stores a whole structured document (the form submission) in one cell.\n- **Drift** — when two places that should agree (form vs database, registry vs code) have quietly gone different.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n- Open `https://blondedca-wq.github.io/5-core-systems/onboarding.html`. In Chrome DevTools → Console, type `SCHEMA_VERSION` and `MACHINES.length`. (v4 on this day; **10** with 17 machines as of Day 17.)\n- Later days found that the function names the v4 form documented did not all exist in the live database — that gap was closed on Day 17. The lesson stands: verify the write from the database, never from the page.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **Registry entry first. Then the machine is real.** Every machine that skipped this (Build 3's hard-coded link, Email Lead Saver, Booking Abandonment) bit later.\n- **\"Would two clients want a different value?\"** is the whole test for what belongs in the form.\n- **A form is a promise.** If the database does not have the function the form calls, the promise is empty — check it from the database side.\n\n## Sources\ngit 1801b0b · ledger §5.5 Aug 13–14 · journal entry 2026-08-13\n"},{"date":"2026-08-17","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"A · Days 3–4 — SMS Rescue, Lead Identifier and Lead Saver ported · Email Lead Capture live","md":"# A · Days 3–4 — SMS Rescue, Lead Identifier and Lead Saver ported · Email Lead Capture live\n\n*Mon Aug 17, 2026 (with Aug 18 for the Meta pages). Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry, git 1e9b16f / 1c10f57 / 817248c and ledger §5.5 — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nTwo roadmap days done in one. The three text-message machines moved onto the platform with a gate in front of every send, the last Google Sheet was retired, and the first **email** machine went live and was trusted enough to send on its own.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters on a sales call\n\n**Three machines ported with Control Gates on every send.** SMS Rescue (the missed-call text-back), Lead Identifier (grades who is texting), Lead Saver (keeps a quiet lead warm). Each now reads its settings from `machine_configs` and passes the gate before any text leaves. *Sales line:* \"Same rules, every machine — I don't have five different safety systems, I have one.\"\n\n**Build 1's Google Sheets log retired.** Supabase only. A client who wants a spreadsheet gets an export view, never the live data.\n\n**Email AI Lead Capture live, on autonomous auto-send.** Inbound email to the business inbox → the AI reads it → a lead is created → a reply goes out with no human in the loop. First machine trusted to run in *Let it run* mode from day one. *Sales line:* \"A quote request that lands at 9pm gets answered at 9pm.\"\n\n**Meta app \"SecondRing DM Rescue\" created** on the developer profile, and **privacy-policy.html + data-deletion.html published** (817248c) because Meta's app review requires both pages to exist at public URLs.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **Port** — move a machine onto the platform without changing what it does for the customer.\n- **Autonomous / Let it run** — the machine sends without asking. The other modes: *Ask me* (approval), *Watch* (observe, log but never send), *Off*.\n- **Export view** — a read-only window onto the data for a client who wants a spreadsheet; the truth stays in the database.\n- **App review** — Meta's process before an app may read or send Instagram / Messenger messages for a business. Needs a privacy policy and a data-deletion page at minimum.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n- `https://blondedca-wq.github.io/5-core-systems/privacy-policy.html` and `/data-deletion.html` are live — those are the Aug 17–18 pages.\n- In n8n, none of the three ported workflows has a Google Sheets node.\n- In Supabase — the email machine's receipts (it was unpublished again on Sep 6, so the newest rows are from before then):\n```sql\nselect e.event_type, e.summary, e.created_at\nfrom events e join machines m on m.id = e.machine_id\nwhere m.key = 'email_lead_capture' order by e.created_at desc limit 5;\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **Port with the gate, not after.** Every machine that reached the platform on this day arrived with its safety checks already attached.\n- **Autonomous is a decision, not a default.** Email was allowed to auto-send because its worst case (an odd reply to a quote request) is mild. Texts to customers stayed gated.\n- **Meta wants URLs before it wants code.** Publish the policy pages the day you create the app.\n\n## Sources\ngit 1e9b16f, 1c10f57, 817248c · ledger §5.5 Aug 17 · journal entry 2026-08-17\n"},{"date":"2026-08-20","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"Business — SECOND RING registered","md":"# Business — SECOND RING registered\n\n*Thu Aug 20, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry and ledger §5.5 — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nThe day the business became a legal thing. SECOND RING was registered as an Ontario sole proprietorship, which unblocked Meta's business verification, and the server had its first full outage — the one that was diagnosed two days later.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters\n\n**SECOND RING registered.** Ontario sole proprietorship, NAICS 541514 (computer systems design and related services), valid Aug 20 2026 → Aug 19 2031. This is the name on the certificate, the name Meta verifies, and the name that goes on the first contract.\n\n**Meta business verification submitted.** Now that a legal entity existed. (Outcome on Aug 26: rejected — the certificate did not show the address. See that day.)\n\n**n8n full outage.** Every workflow dark. Suspected out-of-memory; confirmed on Aug 22 when the droplet was found to have no swap.\n\n*Why a client cares:* \"You are contracting with a registered Ontario business, and the texts your customers get come from a number owned by that business.\"\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **Sole proprietorship** — the simplest business form: you and the business are the same legal person. Registration gives the name and a business number; it does not give the liability shield a corporation does.\n- **NAICS** — the industry code governments use. 541514 is the software / systems design bucket.\n- **Business verification (Meta)** — Meta checking that a real registered business is behind an app before letting it talk to customers on Instagram or Messenger.\n- **OOM (out of memory)** — the server ran out of RAM and the operating system killed the biggest process, which was n8n.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n- The registration certificate (PDF) is the source for the name, the business number and the dates — keep it where the solicitor can see it.\n- Meta Business Manager → Security Centre shows the verification status and, when rejected, the reason.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **Register before you need it.** Meta verification waited ten days on this.\n- **The certificate has to show the address** or Meta rejects it. Read the rejection reason before resubmitting.\n- **An outage with no error is a memory problem** until proven otherwise (Aug 22).\n\n## Sources\nledger §5.5 Aug 20 · journal entry 2026-08-20\n"},{"date":"2026-08-21","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"A · Day 5 — Review Collector standardised + Booking Abandonment live","md":"# A · Day 5 — Review Collector standardised + Booking Abandonment live\n\n*Fri Aug 21, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry and ledger §5.5 — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nTwo things: the Review Engine stopped being a one-off and became the **template** every catalogue machine is built from, and a brand-new machine — Booking Abandonment — went live. The inbound router also got its final order of operations.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters on a sales call\n\n**Review Engine = catalogue template.** Config from the registry, gate on every send, receipts in `events`, a console tile. Every machine after this copied that shape.\n\n**Booking Abandonment live.** Someone starts booking (a call, a form, a text) and goes quiet. A sweep runs every 15 minutes, finds bookings that have aged past their maturity threshold, and sends a gated nudge — refused inside quiet hours, sent when they lift. *Sales line:* \"Half your bookings die in the gap between 'I'll get back to you' and never. This chases the gap for you, politely, and only when it's a decent hour.\" (On Day 16 this exact behaviour became the best demo artifact: refused at 07:45 for quiet hours, sent at 08:00:42.)\n\n**Router chain settled: STOP → Review → Booking → triage.** Every inbound text is checked in that order: is it an opt-out? a review reply? a booking reply? otherwise, grade it as a lead. Order matters — STOP has to win.\n\n**Supabase test data purged.** Real names and numbers from earlier test rounds removed.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **Template machine** — the reference build. New machines copy it, so they inherit its safety and its console tile.\n- **Sweep** — a job that wakes up on a timer, looks for work, does it, goes back to sleep. Booking Abandonment sweeps every 15 minutes.\n- **Maturity threshold** — how old something has to be before it is worth acting on. A booking must age (~an hour) before it is nudged, or you chase people who are still typing.\n- **Triage** — sorting an incoming message into hot / warm / cold / spam.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n```sql\nselect e.event_type, e.guardrail_key, e.created_at\nfrom events e join machines m on m.id = e.machine_id\nwhere m.key = 'booking_abandonment'\norder by e.created_at desc limit 20;\n```\nYou should see `gate.blocked` (quiet_hours) rows followed by `gate.passed` + `nudge_sent` rows — the refuse-then-send pattern.\n\nNote for the record: on Day 16 it was found that Booking Abandonment had **no registry entry** despite being live since today — fixed in registry v10 on Day 17.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **Live is not the same as registered.** This machine ran for three weeks before the form knew it existed.\n- **STOP goes first in the router.** Always.\n- **Let things age before you chase.** A nudge too early looks like spam; a nudge at the right time looks like service.\n\n## Sources\nledger §5.5 Aug 21 · journal entry 2026-08-21\n"},{"date":"2026-08-22","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"A · Day 6 — Voice Receptionist I · the droplet OOM diagnosed","md":"# A · Day 6 — Voice Receptionist I · the droplet OOM diagnosed\n\n*Sat Aug 22, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry, git a22b50c / fb4c274 and ledger §5.5 — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nThe day meant to start the voice receptionist turned into a server day. The droplet had been quietly killing n8n's task runner for three days, and until that was fixed nothing else could be trusted. Day 6 ended 0/4 on the card; the voice bridge moved to Day 7. Not a wasted day — the fix is why everything after it stayed up.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters on a sales call\n\n**The OOM diagnosed.** The droplet has 961 MiB of RAM and had **no swap**. When n8n plus its task runner plus Postgres plus Caddy exceeded that, Linux's out-of-memory killer picked the biggest process — the task runner — and killed it, silently, over and over. Fix: a **2 GB swap file**. Swap is slower than RAM, but it turns a crash into a slowdown.\n\n*Sales line (for the \"is this reliable?\" question):* \"It runs on a server I monitor. When it ran out of memory in August I found it, fixed it and it hasn't happened since — that's the job you're paying me for.\"\n\n**5 Core changes the same day.** The Lovable bill, a new grocery list and the Quran study block were added to the personal app, and the off-day schedule became prayer-anchored: work Fajr → Asr, study Asr → Maghrib.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **RAM vs swap** — RAM is the workbench; swap is the floor space next to it. When the bench is full, the OS puts things on the floor instead of throwing them out the window.\n- **OOM killer** — the part of Linux that, when memory runs out, kills a process to save the machine. It picks the biggest one and does not tell you.\n- **Task runner** — the separate n8n process that actually executes workflow code. If it dies, workflows queue and then fail.\n- **Card ended 0/4** — none of the four roadmap boxes for the day ticked. Recorded honestly; the boxes moved, they were not faked.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\nOn the droplet:\n```\nfree -h            # Swap: 2.0Gi\nswapon --show      # /swapfile 2G\ndmesg | grep -i \"out of memory\"   # empty since Aug 22\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **A $6 server with no swap will kill your biggest process** and never tell you. Add swap the day you rent the box.\n- **A silent crash is a memory problem until proven otherwise** (Aug 20's outage was this).\n- **Record 0/4 as 0/4.** The honest card is what made the Day 7 fix visible.\n\n## Sources\ngit a22b50c, fb4c274 · ledger §5.5 Aug 22 · journal entry 2026-08-22\n"},{"date":"2026-08-23","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"A · Day 7 — the ConversationRelay voice bridge goes live","md":"# A · Day 7 — the ConversationRelay voice bridge goes live\n\n*Sun Aug 23, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry, git 4039e9f / 38472b8 / 0b4ae05 and ledger §5.5 — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nThe first day a machine **talked**. `sr-voice-bridge` answers the business number, listens, thinks with Claude, and speaks back — in real time. Plus a registry version, a Meta surprise, and the second standing rule.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters on a sales call\n\n**The voice bridge, live on +1 226 917 8408.** Twilio's *ConversationRelay* turns a phone call into a stream of text: caller speaks → Twilio transcribes → the bridge (a small Node service on the droplet, behind `auto.secondring.ca`) sends it to Claude → the reply is spoken back. It sits at `/relay/incoming`; if the bridge is down, Twilio plays a hosted \"sorry\" message instead of dead air.\n\n*Sales line:* \"Call the number. That's not a recording, it's the receptionist — ask it something about your furnace.\" (Since Day 9 it also handles barge-in, silence, voicemail and STOP; since Day 16 the silence timer is 15 s, not 6.)\n\n**The transfer problem solved.** Getting code onto the droplet had been painful. Answer: edit in the GitHub web editor, then `git clone` / `git pull` on the server. No file uploads, no copy-paste, a history for every change.\n\n**Registry v5.** The voice machine's settings — greeting, hours text, emergency policy, escalation number, silence timer — went into the onboarding registry.\n\n**Meta App Review found never submitted.** The Aug 20 verification had gone in; the *app review* had not. All text sections were filled in today. (It is still the long pole: business verification was rejected on Aug 26.)\n\n**Registry audit caught a miss:** Email Lead Identifier had shipped with no registry entry. Exactly the failure the first standing rule exists for.\n\n**Owner Console spec written + the second standing rule saved:** *every machine ships a console tile* — registry entry, a plain-English blurb with 30-day counters, and a control notch wired to the machine's real on/off and mode. \"Zero and unbuilt must never look the same on a client's screen\": a tile with no live counter says **\"— not live yet\"**, never `0`.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **ConversationRelay** — Twilio's service that streams a live call as text both ways so software can hold the conversation. $0.07/min.\n- **Bridge** — the small always-on program that connects Twilio to the AI. Ours is a Node service kept alive by systemd.\n- **Fallback** — what the caller hears if the bridge is down. Never silence.\n- **Console tile** — the card on the owner's dashboard for one machine: what it does, what it did in the last 30 days, and its switch.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n- Call **+1 226 917 8408** and ask about hours. That is the bridge answering.\n- On the droplet: `systemctl status sr-voice-bridge` — active, and it restarts itself in about a second if killed (proved Day 9).\n- In Supabase: `select started_at, duration_sec, grade, outcome from voice_calls order by started_at desc limit 5;`\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **Ship code through git, even to one server.** The \"transfer problem\" disappeared the day this was decided.\n- **A machine is not done until the owner can see it.** The second standing rule came out of realising the console had no tile for the newest machine.\n- **Verification and app review are two different Meta queues.** Submitting one is not submitting the other.\n\n## Sources\ngit 4039e9f, 38472b8, 0b4ae05 · ledger §5.5 Aug 23 · journal entry 2026-08-23\n"},{"date":"2026-08-24","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"Evenings — the DM bridge (Aug 24–25)","md":"# Evenings — the DM bridge (Aug 24–25)\n\n*Mon Aug 24 – Tue Aug 25, 2026, evenings after 12-hour shifts. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry and ledger §5.5 — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nTwo evenings, one small service: `sr-dm-bridge`, the program that receives Instagram DMs from Meta and hands them to the DM Rescue machine. Built on the droplet, webhook verified with Meta, and OAuth completed on the demo Instagram account.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters on a sales call\n\n**`sr-dm-bridge` built.** A second always-on Node service on the droplet (port 8090, next to the voice bridge on 8080). Meta POSTs each new message to it; it writes the message to the database and lets the machine classify and, when allowed, reply.\n\n**Meta webhook verified.** Meta sends a challenge to your URL and expects it echoed back; the bridge passed. The messages / comments / message_edit / message_reactions fields were subscribed.\n\n**OAuth completed on `@dakhilplumbingdemo`.** The demo Instagram account granted the app permission to read and send its DMs — the token the bridge uses.\n\n*Sales line (careful — see the caveat):* \"A DM at midnight gets a reply at midnight, graded as a lead by the morning.\" **Caveat found on Day 16:** Meta only *delivers* webhooks to a **published** app. The bridge is healthy and has never received a real inbound DM; until app review + business verification pass, DM Rescue can only be demoed on seeded data in Watch mode. Say \"modelled\" out loud.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **Webhook verification** — Meta's handshake: \"prove you own this URL by echoing my challenge\".\n- **OAuth** — the account owner clicking \"allow\" so the app gets a token to act on their behalf. The token is the thing that expires or gets revoked (the IG token had to be reconnected on Sep 7).\n- **Published vs development app** — a development-mode app can *call* Meta's API for testers, but Meta will not *push* webhooks to it. Different mechanisms.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n- On the droplet: `systemctl status sr-dm-bridge` — active. If Caddy's routing went stale after an update: `bash /opt/sr-dm-bridge/fix-caddy.sh`.\n- Meta developer dashboard → the app → Webhooks: the four Instagram fields show *subscribed*.\n- In Supabase, DM events so far are seeded or gate probes — there is no real inbound row yet, and that is expected until the app is published.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **Two evenings is enough for a bridge** when the platform already has the gate, the tables and the console.\n- **Healthy plumbing does not mean water is flowing.** The bridge passed every check and still cannot receive a message. Check the delivery side, not just your side.\n- **Do not repeat the Day 16 dead end:** adding an Instagram Tester role does not enable webhooks.\n\n## Sources\nledger §5.5 Aug 24–25 · journal entry 2026-08-24\n"},{"date":"2026-08-26","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"A · Day 8 — Voice III: actions and guardrails","md":"# A · Day 8 — Voice III: actions and guardrails\n\n*Wed Aug 26, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry, git 2847af0 / ab4edcd and ledger §5.5 — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nThe receptionist learned to **do** things, not just talk — and the day exposed a bug that had been lying in the database since the beginning: the *Watch* mode had never actually been settable. Also the day Meta said no.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters on a sales call\n\n**Schema v6 → v7: `observe` made canonical.** Every machine has four modes: autonomous (Let it run), approval_required (Ask me), observe (Watch), paused (Off). The database CHECK constraint only accepted the *old* spelling `observe_only`, so any attempt to set a machine to `observe` was refused — meaning **Watch had never been settable** from the console or the form. v7 fixed the constraint and kept the legacy value working.\n\n*Why this matters on a sales call:* the whole risk-free trial pitch — \"run it in Watch mode for two weeks, it logs what it *would* have sent and sends nothing\" — depends on Watch being real. Today is the day it became possible; Day 13 is the day the gate started enforcing it for every machine.\n\n**`sr_gate` invoked by a real machine for the first time.** Until now the shared gate function existed but no machine called it. The voice receptionist's actions (booking, escalation, texting a confirmation) now go through it. \"Built\" and \"called\" became the same thing for one machine.\n\n**Meta business verification rejected** — the certificate did not show the business address. The DM machine stays on seeded data until this is resolved.\n\n**Voice actions.** The receptionist can now act on a call — open a job, escalate to the owner's number — and anything it sends afterwards goes through the gate.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **CHECK constraint** — a database rule that refuses a value that is not on its list. Great when the list is right; invisible when it is stale.\n- **Canonical value** — the one official spelling (`observe`), with older spellings tolerated but never written again.\n- **`sr_gate`** — the one function every send goes through. It returns *ok* or a reason (`observe_only`, `quiet_hours`, `needs_approval`, `contact_opted_out`, `daily_cap`…).\n- **Escalation** — handing a call or message to a human, with the reason logged.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n```sql\nselect public.sr_mode('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000','voice_receptionist');   -- one of the four modes\nselect public.sr_check_send('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000','voice_receptionist','+15005550117','contact',null,'hello')->>'reason';\n```\nSet the machine to Watch on the console and the second query answers `observe_only`. That is the v7 fix working.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **A mode nobody has ever set is a mode that might not work.** Flip every mode on a real machine at least once (this is roadmap box bA-3 from Day 1 — it was the right box).\n- **A function that exists but is never called proves nothing.** \"Built\" ≠ \"called\".\n- **Meta reads the certificate literally.** No address on the document, no verification.\n\n## Sources\ngit 2847af0, ab4edcd · ledger §5.5 Aug 26 · journal entry 2026-08-26\n"},{"date":"2026-08-27","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"A · Day 9 — Voice IV: hardening","md":"# A · Day 9 — Voice IV: hardening\n\n*Thu Aug 27, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry and ledger §5.5 — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nThe receptionist went from \"works on a good call\" to \"survives a bad one\". Every way a real phone call goes wrong got a handler, every call got a receipt with a confidence score, and the service was proved to come back from being killed.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters on a sales call\n\n**Barge-in.** The caller interrupts mid-sentence → the receptionist stops talking and listens. Without this it sounds like a robot reading a script.\n\n**Silence handling.** Nobody speaks → a re-prompt (\"are you still there?\") → hang up cleanly after repeated silence. (Day 16 found the timer was set to 6 seconds against a 10-second script, so it hung up on callers; fixed to 15 with an 8–60 range.)\n\n**Voicemail detection.** The receptionist realises it has reached a machine and does not hold a conversation with a beep.\n\n**STOP respected.** A caller who has opted out of texts does not get the follow-up text the receptionist would normally send. The gate's first check, honoured on the voice path.\n\n**`voice_call_logged` with `ai_confidence`.** Every call writes a receipt: transcript, caller name, service, urgency, grade, outcome, and how sure the AI was. Low confidence is what routes a call to a human.\n\n**kill -9 → back in ~1 s.** The service was killed the hard way and systemd restarted it in about a second. *Sales line:* \"If the receptionist's program crashes, it is back before the next ring.\"\n\n**Two bugs fixed.** The *ask-me deadlock* (commit 1540bf4 — as best the record shows, a call in Ask-me mode could sit waiting on an approval that could not arrive during the call) and `sr_tenant_by_number` now matching on digits only, so a number formatted with spaces or a `+` still finds the right business.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **Barge-in** — talking over the system and being heard.\n- **`ai_confidence`** — a 0–1 number the model gives for how sure it is. Below a threshold → escalate to a person.\n- **systemd** — the Linux service manager. \"Restart=always\" is the setting that brought the bridge back in a second.\n- **Deadlock** — two things each waiting for the other, forever. Here: the call waiting for approval, the approval waiting for the call to end.\n- **Digits-only match** — strip everything but numbers before comparing phone numbers, so `+1 (226) 917-8408` equals `12269178408`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n- Call the number, interrupt it mid-sentence — it stops. Say nothing — it re-prompts, then ends the call politely.\n- On the droplet: `sudo kill -9 $(pgrep -f sr-voice-bridge)` then `systemctl status sr-voice-bridge` a second later — active again.\n- In Supabase: `select caller_name, urgency, grade, outcome, escalated from voice_calls order by started_at desc limit 5;` — plus the matching `voice_call_logged` rows in `events` with `ai_confidence` filled.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **Hardening is the demo.** Nobody is impressed that it talks; they are impressed that it handles being interrupted, ignored and hung up on.\n- **Every call is a receipt.** The confidence number is what makes \"it escalates when unsure\" a true claim.\n- **Prove the restart by killing it.** Then you can say it out loud.\n\n## Sources\nledger §5.5 Aug 27 · journal entry 2026-08-27\n"},{"date":"2026-08-31","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"A · Day 10 — DM Rescue on the Meta test app (observe)","md":"# A · Day 10 — DM Rescue on the Meta test app (observe)\n\n*Mon Aug 31 → Tue Sep 1, 2026 (Days 10 and 11 ran together). Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry and ledger §5.5 — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nThe Instagram machine got its brain. On top of the DM bridge from Aug 24–25, DM Rescue now reads a message, classifies it, qualifies the sender and files them as a graded lead. It ships in **observe** (Watch) mode on purpose: it decides what it *would* reply and logs it, but sends nothing until Meta approval lands.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters on a sales call\n\n**The pipeline: webhook → classify → qualify → contact.** A DM arrives → the AI decides what kind of message it is (quote request, question, spam, existing customer) → asks itself the qualifying questions (what service, how urgent, where) → writes or updates a contact with a lead grade. All of it is logged with the AI's confidence and reasoning.\n\n**Observe-only, deliberately.** Meta had rejected business verification on Aug 26 and the app is unpublished, so no real DM can arrive yet. Rather than wait, the machine was built and parked in Watch: every seeded DM produces a \"would have sent\" receipt in the Silence Log. On Day 16, 11 gate calls → 11 holds with reason `observe_only` — proof that Watch is genuinely enforced.\n\n*Sales line:* \"Watch mode is how every machine starts with you. It reads your DMs for two weeks and shows you what it would have said. You flip it on when you trust it.\"\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **Classify** — sort the message into a type.\n- **Qualify** — pull out the facts that decide whether it is a real job (service, urgency, location).\n- **Graded lead** — hot / warm / cold / spam, written on the contact so the owner sees a ranked list, not an inbox.\n- **Observe / Watch** — the mode where the machine does everything except send. Its output is the Silence Log.\n- **Seeded** — data invented to exercise the machine. Every seeded row is tagged `modelled`; the console labels it as such.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n```sql\nselect e.event_type, e.guardrail_key, e.ai_confidence, e.created_at\nfrom events e join machines m on m.id = e.machine_id\nwhere m.key = 'dm_rescue' order by e.created_at desc limit 20;\n```\nExpect `gate.blocked` rows with `guardrail_key = observe_only`. There are no real inbound DMs — 199 seeded events, 11 gate probes as of Day 16 — and there cannot be until the app is published.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **Build it, then park it in Watch.** Waiting on Meta would have cost the machine; Watch cost nothing.\n- **Watch mode is a product feature, not a workaround.** It became the risk-free trial in the pitch.\n- **Do not chase Tester roles.** They let you call the API; they do not make Meta deliver webhooks. App Review + business verification is the only road.\n\n## Sources\nledger §5.5 Aug 31 → Sep 1 · journal entry 2026-08-31\n"},{"date":"2026-09-01","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"A · Day 11 — Customer Reactivation runner + registry v8/v9","md":"# A · Day 11 — Customer Reactivation runner + registry v8/v9\n\n*Tue Sep 1, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry, git 03e28f5 / 7af0b1a / 47726cd / c1d54b7 / 40bea96 and ledger §5.5 — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nThe first machine that reaches **out** instead of reacting. Customer Reactivation looks at past jobs and proposes a message to customers who have gone quiet — with the anti-spam law hard-coded into it and the owner's approval required by default.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters on a sales call\n\n**`reactivate.js` on the droplet.** A daily runner (10:00 Toronto intended; Day 16 found the box was on UTC so it fired at 06:00 — fix: `CRON_TZ=America/Toronto`). It selects eligible past customers, drafts a message for each, and raises an **approval** rather than sending.\n\n**CASL filter hard-coded.** Canada's anti-spam law only allows a commercial text to someone with consent — express, or implied from a business relationship within a time window. The filter is in the code, not a setting: no consent → not eligible, full stop. *Sales line:* \"It only ever messages people you have done work for, inside the window the law allows, and it shows you the job it is referring to.\"\n\n**Every message justified by a past job.** The draft names the job (\"we serviced your furnace last October…\"). No generic blasts.\n\n**`approval_required` by default.** Each draft sits in the owner's approvals queue. Day 16: \"22 eligible, 5 selected\", 5 approvals raised and held.\n\n**Registry v8 → v9.** v8 added DM Rescue's full config, reactivation, and the win-back stack; v9 added `upsell_engine` — **never auto-sends, approvals only** (built on Day 12).\n\n**5 Core the same day:** workouts moved to a 3-day rotation with last-cycle and PR shown; bills gained edit / delete / add with cloud sync.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **Runner** — a script on the server that runs on a schedule (cron), unlike a webhook machine that waits to be poked.\n- **CASL** — Canada's Anti-Spam Legislation. Express consent, or implied consent from an existing business relationship (with time limits), before a commercial message. Every text must identify the sender and offer an unsubscribe.\n- **Approval** — a row in the `approvals` table: proposed message, reason, expiry. The owner approves, rejects, or edits; only an approved row can pass the gate.\n- **Drain** — the second pass that sends the approved ones (built Day 13).\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n```sql\nselect summary, status, expires_at, created_at from approvals\nwhere action_type ilike '%react%' order by created_at desc limit 10;\n```\nOn the droplet: `crontab -l` shows the reactivation line; `journalctl -u sr-reactivation --since today` (or the runner's log) shows \"N eligible, M selected\".\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **Put the law in the code, not the config.** A client cannot switch CASL off by mistake.\n- **Outbound starts in Ask-me.** Reactivation and Upsell are the two machines that never got autonomous by default, and that is the right call.\n- **A runner's clock is the server's clock.** Check the timezone before trusting the schedule.\n\n## Sources\ngit 03e28f5, 7af0b1a, 47726cd, c1d54b7, 40bea96 · ledger §5.5 · journal entry 2026-09-01\n"},{"date":"2026-09-04","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"A · Day 12 — the AI Upsell Engine","md":"# A · Day 12 — the AI Upsell Engine\n\n*Fri Sep 4, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry and ledger §5.5 — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nThe machine that finds money in work already done. Upsell looks at the photos and notes from a finished job, spots a legitimate add-on (the water heater next to the furnace that is fifteen years old), and drafts a card for a manager to review. It never sends on its own — and the database, not the code, is what stops it.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters on a sales call\n\n**`upsell.js` runner.** Daily (intended 10:15 Toronto). For each recently completed job with media: read the job → look for real, defensible add-on opportunities → write a **manager review card** as an approval. Day 16 run: \"0 jobs with photos, 0 drafted\" — correct behaviour with no fresh input.\n\n**`__approved_send_guard__`.** The gate's approval check: a send in `approval_required` mode passes **only** when an `approvals` row exists with `status = 'approved'` and its id matches the send's subject id. Runners pass the bare approval id. No approved row, no send — regardless of what the runner thinks. *Sales line:* \"It can suggest. Only your manager can send. That is not a policy, it is a lock in the database.\"\n\n**Registry v9** (from Day 11) already described this machine as approvals-only, so the form, the console and the runner agree.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **Upsell** — offering a related, genuinely useful extra on a job that is already happening. The guardrail word is *legitimate*: the draft has to point at something in the photos.\n- **Manager review card** — the approval row rendered on the console: the job, the suggestion, the draft message, approve / edit / reject.\n- **Guard** — a check inside the gate function. `__approved_send_guard__` is its name in the code.\n- **Subject id** — the thing a send is about. For an upsell it is the approval's own id, which is how the gate matches them.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n```sql\nselect summary, status, created_at from approvals\nwhere action_type ilike '%upsell%' order by created_at desc limit 10;\n\n-- an unapproved upsell cannot pass the gate:\nselect public.sr_check_send('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000','upsell_engine','+15005550117','approval','not-a-real-id','test')->>'reason';  -- needs_approval\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **\"Never auto-sends\" must be a database rule** or it is a habit that a future edit can break.\n- **No input, no output is a pass, not a failure.** \"0 drafted\" on a day with no new photos is the machine working.\n- **Upsell drafts must cite the evidence.** That is what makes them defensible when a customer asks \"why are you telling me this?\"\n\n## Sources\nledger §5.5 Sep 4 · journal entry 2026-09-04\n"},{"date":"2026-09-05","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"A · Day 13 — integration day: one OS, not ten scripts","md":"# A · Day 13 — integration day: one OS, not ten scripts\n\n*Sat Sep 5, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry, git 5e114f7 / a2280c3 / 941e1b4 / 7efe986 / f40ce22, ledger §5.5 and the gate contract in the skill (§0.2) — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nThe most important day of Category A. Ten machines had been built one at a time; today they were made to behave as one system. The shared gate was tested the way an inspector tests a panel — every breaker flipped — and it was found to have **three holes**. All three were fixed *at the gate*, so every machine inherited the fix at once.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters on a sales call\n\n**Unified inbox.** `sr_resolve_contact` + `contact_identities`: a customer who called, texted and DM'd is one contact, not three. Every channel lands in one thread.\n\n**The gate regression found three holes:**\n1. **Watch was not enforced** — a machine set to Watch could still send.\n2. **The daily cap never fired** — the count was looking at the wrong rows.\n3. **Phone-less sends skipped the checks** — an Instagram reply (no phone number) bypassed opt-out.\n\nPlus: a mode default that violated its own CHECK, the missing `sr_handle_inbound_keyword` (STOP/START), and phone normalisation to E.164 everywhere.\n\n**The gate contract (now the third standing rule).** Every outbound message from every machine calls `sr_gate` → `sr_check_send`, which runs these checks in order: **1** opt-out (absolute — beats everything, including emergencies) · **2** paused / not installed · **3** Watch · **4** approval required · **5** taken over by a human · **6** quiet hours (emergency keywords bypass this and the cap, never opt-out) · **7** daily cap. Every refusal is logged with its reason — that is the Silence Log.\n\n**Exactly three exemptions, ratified today, all logged:** an *Ask-me* refusal of an owner alert still reaches the owner (it *is* the approval notification); owner numbers are exempt from the daily cap only; the watchdog's ops SMS goes direct.\n\n**Drains shipped** for upsell, reactivation and DM — the second pass that sends what the owner approved. **Watchdog fault-injected** — broken on purpose, alarm confirmed. **Schema 9.**\n\n**The Field Guide** — all 37 machines, in plain English — added as the 🔧 Machines tab on the roadmap page.\n\n*Sales line:* \"Every machine sends through one door. Seven checks, in a fixed order, your opt-outs first. When it holds a message, you can read why.\"\n\n**5 Core the same day:** Quran khatmah tab + surah reader with translation and Ibn Kathir tafsir.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **Regression test** — re-checking things that used to work, to find what quietly broke.\n- **Fix at the gate** — repair the shared function once instead of patching ten machines.\n- **E.164** — the international phone format (`+12269178408`). Normalising to it is why two spellings of one number are one contact.\n- **Drain** — the runner pass that turns approved rows into actual sends.\n- **Exemption** — a named, logged case that bypasses part of the gate. There are three. Anything else that bypasses it is a bug.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n```sql\n-- Watch is enforced (set a machine to Watch on the console first):\nselect public.sr_check_send('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000','dm_rescue',null,'dm','ig:DAY13_probe','hello')->>'reason';   -- observe_only\n\n-- STOP / START round trip on a Twilio magic number:\nselect public.sr_handle_inbound_keyword('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000','+15005550117','STOP','sms_router');\nselect public.sr_check_send('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000','review_engine','+15005550117','contact',null,'how did we do?')->>'reason';   -- contact_opted_out\nselect public.sr_handle_inbound_keyword('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000','+15005550117','START','sms_router');\n```\n`events` is append-only, so probe rows are permanent — use the `day13:*` / `+150055501xx` conventions.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **Test the gate, not the machines.** Three holes in one function meant ten machines were unsafe; one fix made ten machines safe.\n- **Opt-out is absolute.** It sits above emergencies, approvals and caps. Nothing is allowed to argue with it.\n- **Name the exemptions.** Three, written down, logged. Everything else that skips the gate is a bug by definition.\n- **Phone-less is still a send.** A message body with no number counts.\n\n## Sources\ngit 5e114f7, a2280c3, 941e1b4, 7efe986, f40ce22 · ledger §5.5 Sep 5 · skill §0.2 · journal entry 2026-09-05\n"},{"date":"2026-09-06","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"A · Day 14 — the fake company: Fennwick seeded","md":"# A · Day 14 — the fake company: Fennwick seeded\n\n*Sun Sep 6, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry and ledger §5.5 — not written on the day. (A Day 14 guide was written at the time as `study-guide.html`; it was never published and is not in the vault, so this is the record's version.)*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nThe showroom got a tenant. **Fennwick Plumbing & Drain** is a plumbing company that does not exist, with six months of history that reads as if it did: every missed call, every text-back, every review ask, every refusal by the gate. It is what a prospect sees on the console before they ever give you their own data. Also the day one machine was pulled for writing real personal data into that showroom.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters on a sales call\n\n**Tenant Zero = Fennwick.** Seeded Mar 6 → Sep 6: **11,364 events · 1,152 contacts · 734 jobs · 960 leads · 443 calls · 468 review asks · 221 Silence Log refusals.** Every event carries `payload.modelled = true`, and `tenants.is_demo` drives a MODELLED banner and a per-card chip on the console — so the UI computes nothing and pretends nothing.\n\n**PII audit: PASS.** All 1,152 contacts use `555` fictional numbers. No real person is in the showroom.\n\n*Sales line — and the rule that goes with it:* \"Everything on this screen is modelled. Say it out loud in the first ten seconds of every demo.\" A seeded number is never described as a client result.\n\n**Email Lead Saver unpublished.** It had written *real* sender names and addresses from the inbox into the showroom tenant. Pulled the same day; still off as of Day 17 (decision open).\n\n**Lovable credits ran out** → Days 15–16 re-planned Lovable-free. (Credits refresh Sep 20 — hard date.)\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **Seed** — data generated to look like a real history. It exercises every machine and every tile.\n- **Tenant Zero** — the demo tenant. Its id is all zeros (`00000000-…`), which is why the proof queries in these guides use it.\n- **PII** — personally identifiable information: names, numbers, emails, addresses. A showroom must contain none that is real.\n- **MODELLED** — the word on every seeded figure. Global banner + per-card chip, driven by `is_demo`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n```sql\nselect count(*) from events where tenant_id='00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000';               -- ~11.4k+\nselect count(*) from contacts where tenant_id='00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000'\n  and phone_e164 not like '+1%555%';                                                            -- 0 = PII-clean\nselect count(*) filter (where payload->>'modelled'='true') as modelled, count(*) as total\nfrom events where tenant_id='00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000' and created_at > now() - interval '30 days';\n```\nOn Day 16 the last query read 1,922 modelled of 2,050 — so 128 events in the last 30 days were genuinely live.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **A showroom is a tenant with a flag**, not a separate app. Same tables, same gate, same console — plus `is_demo`.\n- **Audit for PII before the first demo, and after any machine that reads a real inbox.** The Email Lead Saver leak is the case study.\n- **\"Modelled\" is a word you say, not just a chip you render.**\n\n## Sources\nledger §5.5 Sep 6 · journal entry 2026-09-06\n"},{"date":"2026-09-07","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"Get-ahead — memory rebuilt · Category B Day 1 built a week early","md":"# Get-ahead — memory rebuilt · Category B Day 1 built a week early\n\n*Mon Sep 7, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry and ledger §5.5 / §5.7 — not written on the day.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nA day off the roadmap, used to get ahead of it. All 34 previous chats were re-read and the skill rebuilt so nothing lived only in someone's memory; a broken Instagram token was reconnected; the plan around the Lovable freeze was settled; and the first day of Category B — the rate card — was built a week before its date. One paste did not run, and that became the Day 15 lesson.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters\n\n**Memory rebuilt.** 34 chats → one skill (`ai-trade-business-os`), with a dated history ledger (§5.5), your working context (§5.6) and a get-ahead lane (§5.7). Every session since starts from it.\n\n**IG token reconnected.** The Instagram OAuth token had lapsed; the DM bridge could not have replied even in principle. Reconnected.\n\n**Lovable refresh fixed at Sep 20 (hard).** The §5.7 lane agreed: what can be built ahead without Lovable, what only you can do (solicitor, sign-ins, DDL pastes), and what has lead time.\n\n**Numbers researched and banked** — vision model cost per photo, storage, and CPA figures — so nobody re-researches them. (§5.7 \"Already researched — do NOT re-research\".)\n\n**Category B Day 1 built early.** Rate card DDL, proof kit, registry patch, quote template, pricing engine spec, solicitor brief, cost sheet.\n\n**The DDL paste did not run.** You pasted the rate-card DDL into Supabase and it reported success. It had not run at all — 0 of 9 tables. Cause: Supabase's **\"Potential issues detected\"** dialog silently cancels the run unless you click through (**\"Run without RLS\"**), and if any text is selected the button reads **\"Run selected\"** and only that runs. Found on Day 15, when the tables were checked from the database side.\n\n*Why it matters on a sales call:* the rate card is what turns a photo into a priced quote in Category B. The pricing engine's *guarantee* — same inputs, same price, every time — was the thing built here and proved on Day 15 (128/128 offline, 30/30 live).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **DDL** — the SQL that creates tables and functions (as opposed to reading or writing rows). Only you paste DDL.\n- **Rate card** — the client's price list as structured data: services, units, prices, overlap rules. Nine tables.\n- **Proof kit** — the queries that prove a piece of work landed, written before the work is pasted.\n- **Solicitor brief** — what the lawyer needs to review before the first paid contract, and the three questions to ask.\n- **Get-ahead lane** — work that can be done before its roadmap date without blocking on anyone.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n- Skill §5.7 lists the banked cost numbers — read them before researching anything about vision, storage or CPA.\n- The rate card proof: `select count(*) from pg_class where relname like 'rate_card%';` — 0 on Sep 7 (the paste that did not run), 9 after Sep 9.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **\"All rows passed\" on screen is not a write.** Re-query `pg_class` / the table itself afterwards, always.\n- **Check the button says plain \"Run\"** — not \"Run selected\", not a dialog waiting to be clicked.\n- **Rebuild memory before it is needed.** The Sep 7 skill is why Days 15–17 could run on a phone call's worth of context.\n\n## Sources\nledger §5.5 Sep 7 · skill §5.7 · journal entry 2026-09-07\n"},{"date":"2026-09-09","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"reconstructed","source":"reconstructed Sep 15 2026 from the log, git refs and the ledger","title":"A · Day 15 — contract day: the rate card and the pricing engine land","md":"# A · Day 15 — contract day: the rate card and the pricing engine land\n\n*Wed Sep 9, 2026. Reconstructed on Sep 15 from the log entry, git f001645 / 1b1e8fa and ledger §5.5 — not written on the day. Day 16's findings (attached to Sep 10) audit this day honestly: its four roadmap boxes were not the things that landed; the rate card and the pricing engine were real.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the day was\n\nThe day the Category B foundation actually reached the database — after discovering Monday's paste had never run — and the day the roadmap was audited against the Lovable freeze. Chrome's extension was offline at the start, so public pages were worked in the built-in browser.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What changed, and why it matters on a sales call\n\n**Roadmap audit.** Only **4** future boxes in the entire roadmap need Lovable and 3 are October or later; the one blocking box was the stale Day 15 card itself. **f027064 shipped:** Day 15 rewritten as a contract day with no Lovable, Day 16 given the first real DM drain send + a site honesty pass, B Day 1 marked banked early, B Day 3 retitled to wiring, B Day 5 noting the shared Sep 20, and the \"Every system, scored\" table corrected from 15 stale rows to 18 true ones (10 DONE). Step counts unchanged (66/60), so your ticked boxes stayed aligned.\n\n**Pricing engine built and proven 128/128 offline** — every combination in the test matrix priced the same way every time.\n\n**`b1-rate-card.sql` finally landed** — 9 tables with RLS, `btree_gist` (the extension that lets the database refuse overlapping price ranges), the overlap guard — **24/24**. Then **conformance proved 30/30 against the live `sr_price_line`**: the offline engine and the live database price identically.\n\n**Two SQL determinism bugs** found by the matrix and fixed (`b2-sql-determinism-fix.sql`, 4/4) — the *guarantee* changed (same input → same output, always) without a single price moving.\n\n**Onboarding:** trade service templates and rate-card intake added to the form (Fennwick 25 services; Grand River 46 later).\n\n**Posture verified clean; zero synthetic rows left.**\n\n*Sales line:* \"Your price list is in the system as rules, not a PDF. Two people quoting the same job get the same number, and the database will not let two prices overlap.\"\n\n---\n\n## 3. Plain-English glossary\n\n- **Determinism** — same inputs, same output, every time. A pricing engine that is not deterministic is a dice roll with a logo.\n- **Conformance** — the offline engine and the live database agreeing on every test line.\n- **`btree_gist` / overlap guard** — a Postgres extension that lets a table rule say \"no two rows may cover the same range\", so a price band cannot be entered twice.\n- **Honesty pass** — editing a card so it claims only what actually happened.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How to check it yourself\n\n```sql\nselect extname from pg_extension where extname='btree_gist';                       -- present\nselect count(*) from pg_class where relname like 'rate_card%';                       -- 9 (plus rate_card_templates)\nselect sr_current_schema_version();                                                  -- 9 on this day; 10 since Day 17\n```\nRun the 30-line conformance matrix from the Day 15 proof kit against `sr_price_line` — it prints PASS per line.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **Verify the write from the database, not the screen.** Monday's \"success\" was nothing. Today's `pg_class` query was the truth.\n- **A test matrix finds what a demo never will.** Two determinism bugs, zero price changes.\n- **Audit the roadmap when a dependency freezes.** Four boxes needed Lovable, not forty — the plan barely moved once it was counted.\n\n## Sources\ngit f001645, 1b1e8fa · ledger §5.5 Sep 9 · journal entry 2026-09-09\n"},{"date":"2026-09-10","group":"work","kind":"findings","label":"Findings","origin":"original","source":"project doc claude/day-16-findings.md","title":"Day 16 — Thu Sep 10, 2026 — findings","md":"# Day 16 — Thu Sep 10, 2026 — findings\n\nEverything below was read off the live Supabase database (`tjmxqyuzglcfnhgwamit`) or observed on a\nreal phone call. Nothing here comes from the roadmap, the registry, or memory.\n\n---\n\n## CORRECTION — an earlier version of this doc claimed n8n was dead. It is not.\n\n**Retracted claim:** \"every n8n-hosted machine has been dark since Sep 7; n8n executes nothing.\"\n\n**What actually happened.** A synthetic `booking_attempts` row was created 06:45:51 via\n`sr_booking_started` on `+15005550116`. Nothing touched it for 24 minutes, across three quarter-hour\nsweep boundaries, so it was called dead. Then:\n\n```\n07:45:43  booking_abandonment  gate.blocked  quiet_hours  send a booking nudge -> quiet_hours\n07:45:43  booking_abandonment  nudge_denied               Gate denied nudge rung 1 to +15005550116\n08:00:42  booking_abandonment  gate.passed   ok           send a booking nudge -> ok\n08:00:43  booking_abandonment  nudge_sent                 Nudge rung 1 sent to +15005550116\n```\n\n**The error:** an abandoned booking must *age* before it is worth nudging — about an hour, going by\n06:45 → 07:45. The sweep was running the whole time; the test row simply was not ripe. Absence of\nevents was read as \"not running\" when for the other machines it meant **nothing to do** — Fennwick's\nseed ends Sep 6, so there are no new missed calls, no new web forms, no newly completed jobs, and a\nmachine with no input logs nothing.\n\n**Lesson worth keeping:** on this platform, silence in `events` is not evidence of failure. Every\nmachine is input-driven and most inputs stopped when the seed ended. To test a machine, give it a\nripe input and wait past its own maturity threshold — do not infer from the log being quiet.\n\n**Still open, but much less alarming:** the watchdog logged zero events in 10 days. Probably nothing\nto alert on. Worth confirming it watches anything at all.\n\n---\n\n## Machines with live evidence TODAY (5 of 10)\n\n| machine | evidence |\n|---|---|\n| `voice_receptionist` | real call 06:29:22–06:31:15, job opened — but see the silence bug below |\n| `dm_rescue` | 11 gate calls, 11 holds, reason `observe_only` — Watch genuinely enforced |\n| `customer_reactivation` | 06:00 run: *\"22 eligible, 5 selected\"*, 5 approvals raised and held |\n| `upsell_engine` | 06:15 run executed; reported *\"0 jobs with photos, 0 drafted\"* (no fresh input) |\n| `booking_abandonment` | full nudge cycle: refused at 07:45 for quiet hours, sent at 08:00:42 |\n\nThe booking_abandonment run is the best single demo artifact of the day: the machine had a customer\nto chase at 07:45 and **refused**, then went at 08:00 when quiet hours lifted.\n\n---\n\n## THE REAL BREAK — the voice receptionist hangs up on callers\n\nAhmed's report: *\"it asked if i was still there every time it talked without waiting, and hung up\nafter only 5 seconds no speaking, and when i asked it something different it just hung up.\"*\n\n**Cause: `silence_reprompt_seconds = 6`** against its own script lengths:\n\n| config | chars | ~seconds to speak |\n|---|---|---|\n| `greeting` | 72 | ~4.5 |\n| `hours_text` | 148 | ~9 |\n| `emergency_policy` | 163 | ~10 |\n\nOn the greeting the caller gets ~1.5s before the machine decides they have gone. On `hours_text` or\n`emergency_policy` **the timer expires while the machine is still speaking** — it talks itself into\na hangup, which is exactly what happened when Ahmed asked an off-script question.\n\nThree silences then hangup — 06:29:28, :39, :50, hangup :56. Confirmed twice in one call.\n\n`silence_reprompt_seconds` is the **only one of the 16 voice settings with no min, no max and no\ndescription** — precisely the hole the onboarding standing rule exists to catch.\n\nAlso: `voice_silence_hangup` fired at 06:29:56 and the call continued another 74 seconds, so the\nfirst hangup did not take. And `voice_barge_in` was logged twice in the same second at 06:29:40.\n\n**Open question the config bump answers:** whether the timer runs from when the machine *starts*\nspeaking or when it *finishes*. Bump to 15, make a test call, read the gaps between `voice_silence`\nevents. ~15s flat means it runs from speech start and the bridge code needs fixing; ~20s+ means it\nalready resets on speech end and 6 was simply too small.\n\n---\n\n## Gate: emergency keywords match on substrings\n\n`emergency_keywords` = gas, leak, flood, flooding, smoke, fire, sparking, burning, no heat,\ncarbon monoxide, sewage. Matching is plain substring, proven live with four probes:\n\n| message | flagged emergency | via |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"customer asked about a **gasket** replacement\" | yes | `gas` |\n| \"one exterior **floodlight** on the soffit\" | yes | `flood` |\n| \"the boiler **fired** up fine\" | yes | `fire` |\n| \"no breakage on the fittings\" | no | control |\n| \"confirming your appointment for Tuesday\" | no | control |\n\nEmergency bypasses **quiet hours and the daily cap** (never opt-out). So routine messages mentioning\na gasket send at 3am and ignore the 4-per-day limit. Fix is word-boundary matching inside\n`sr_check_send`. Do **not** narrow the keyword list instead — a false negative on a real gas leak is\nfar worse than a false positive.\n\n---\n\n## `sms_router` has no row in the `machines` table\n\nThe registry lists it live/autonomous. The database has no such machine. So it has no\n`tenant_machines` row and cannot be paused, watched, gated per tenant, or shown on the console — and\nit is the inbound webhook routing **STOP → Review Engine → Booking Rescue → lead triage**.\n\n`sr_handle_inbound_keyword` takes a `machine_key`. There are **16 events with a null `machine_id`**\non this tenant. Opt-out is the one absolute guardrail, so the STOP path needs an end-to-end live test\nwith a magic number.\n\n## Posture: 27 unbuilt machines read \"Let it run\"\n\nNot 6 as recorded. B_sales 6, C_operations 6, D_employee 5, E_qc 4, F_intelligence 6 — all\n`enabled=true, mode=autonomous`. Breaks the second standing rule.\n\n**Careful with the fix:** filtering on \"not Category A\" is wrong — `estimate_ladder` is in `B_sales`\nand has **544 events, last Sep 5**. It is Build 5 and it genuinely runs. Filter on \"has never logged\nan event\" instead, which pauses 26, not 27.\n\n`machines` has **no status column**, so the database cannot tell \"switched off\" from \"never built\".\n\n## Five functions missing, one of them new\n\n`sr_onboard_tenant`, `sr_tenant_config`, `sr_send_log`, `sr_tenants_missing` — known.\n**`sr_set_tenant_status` — new.** That is the Owner Console's one-tap master switch. Never written.\n\n## Approvals are rotting, because pg_cron was never installed\n\n24 pending, **14 already past `expires_at`**. `sr_expire_approvals()` exists and has never been\ncalled — and **`pg_cron` is not installed on this project at all**, so there is no scheduler inside\nPostgres. It is available to install.\n\n## Both runners are on UTC, not Toronto\n\nReactivation fires 06:00 Toronto (documented 10:00). Upsell fires 06:15 (documented 10:15). Both land\n**inside quiet hours**. Harmless only because both ship `approval_required` and stop before the\nquiet-hours check. Flip either to autonomous and it texts customers at six in the morning. Fix with\n`CRON_TZ=America/Toronto` at the top of the droplet crontab.\n\n## Registry vs database drift, measured\n\n- Live registry: `SCHEMA_VERSION` 9, nine machines. Database: **37 machines**.\n- Keys disagree: registry `build1_missed_call` / `build2_lead_triage` / `build3_review` vs database\n  `missed_call_rescue` / `speed_to_lead` / `review_engine`. The database keys are canonical.\n- `build2_lead_triage` still carries mode `\"approval\"`, which the DB CHECK does not allow.\n- `review_engine` is `autonomous` in the database and `observe` in the registry.\n- The real ten-machine fleet is Category `A_revenue`, which includes `missed_appointment` and\n  `email_lead_capture` and does **not** include `sms_router` or `estimate_ladder`.\n\n## Roadmap claims Day 15 was 4/4; it was 0/4\n\nNo tenant #2 (the function doesn't exist), no console views (0 of 5), `sr_expire_approvals`\nunscheduled, `study-guide.html` still unpublished. Day 15 did land the rate card and the pricing\nengine, which are real — but they weren't these four. The 4/4 comes from `lab.roadmap.done.v1` in\nthe browser (88 ticked boxes), not from the HTML.\n\n---\n\n## Confirmed good\n\n- Rate card genuinely live: 9/9 tables, `btree_gist`, 5 `sr_booking_*` functions,\n  `sr_current_schema_version()` = 9. Plus an undocumented 10th table, `rate_card_templates`\n  (29 public tables, not 28).\n- **Silence Log is demo-grade.** 30 days: 424 sent, 55 held — quiet_hours 25, observe_only 14,\n  needs_approval 13, opted_out 1, daily_cap 1, taken_over 1. All six reasons present in real data.\n- Fennwick is PII-clean: all 1,152 contacts use `555` fictional numbers.\n\n## Schema notes worth keeping\n\n- `contacts.phone_e164`, not `phone`.\n- `events` has `created_at`, no `occurred_at`. Seeded rows carry `payload.modelled = true`;\n  1,922 of 2,050 in the last 30 days are modelled, so 128 are genuinely live.\n- `events.subject_type` is polymorphic — job/lead/contact/voice_call/booking_attempt/dm/reactivation/\n  review/upsell. Only 37 of 82 `contact`-typed rows resolve by direct uuid join, so any attribution\n  must go through `sr_subject_contact(tenant, subject_type, subject_id)`.\n- Postgres is 17.6, so views support `security_invoker = true` — required, or the console views\n  bypass RLS and leak across tenants.\n- Never verify a write inside the statement that performed it; the sibling sub-select reads the\n  snapshot from statement start.\n"},{"date":"2026-09-10","group":"work","kind":"startup","label":"Startup prompt (Day 17)","origin":"original","source":"project doc claude/day-17-startup-prompt.md","title":"Startup prompt — next session (after Day 16, Thu Sep 10 2026)","md":"# Startup prompt — next session (after Day 16, Thu Sep 10 2026)\n\nPaste this at the start of the next session.\n\n---\n\nLoad the `ai-trade-business-os` skill first. Day 16 is done. Here is what changed, all of it\nverified against the live database rather than reported:\n\n**Fixed and shipped Day 16**\n\n- **Voice receptionist was hanging up on callers.** `silence_reprompt_seconds` was 6 against a\n  10-second script, so the timer expired while the machine was still speaking. Now **15**, with\n  min 8 / max 60 guard rails. Proven on a second live call: gaps went 6s → 15–16s and the clock\n  resets when the caller speaks. **Residual bug:** the timer still starts when the machine *begins*\n  speaking, not when it finishes, so on `emergency_policy` (~10s) a caller gets only ~5s. One-line\n  fix in `/opt/sr-voice-bridge` — start the clock on utterance end. Not a blocker; it's polish.\n- **Posture:** 26 never-run machines set `enabled=false, mode='paused'`. All ten `A_revenue`\n  untouched. `estimate_ladder` deliberately left ON — it has 544 events and genuinely runs.\n- **Approvals:** `sr_expire_approvals()` returned 14. **`pg_cron` was never installed** — now\n  installed, job 1 sweeps hourly.\n- **Five console views live** — `v_console_fleet / money_wall / receipt / silence / approvals`,\n  `security_invoker = true` verified on all five.\n- **Money Wall: 159 jobs, $114,715**, led by `missed_call_rescue` at $72,855, of 596 completed\n  jobs worth $409,785.\n- **Site honesty pass shipped and verified live** (`be2d30e`). Day 15's card no longer claims\n  four things that never happened; five scoreboard rows corrected. Step counts unchanged (16\n  blocks, 4 per card) so localStorage progress stays aligned.\n- **STOP/START proven end to end.** STOP set `opted_out_at`, gate refused with\n  `contact_opted_out`; START cleared it, gate returned `ok`.\n\n**Open bugs, in priority order**\n\n1. ~~**Emergency keywords match on substrings.**~~ **FIXED Sep 10.** The bug was in\n   `sr_is_emergency`, not `sr_check_send` — it used `position(lower(kw) in hay) > 0`, plain\n   substring. Replaced with word-boundary regex: `hay ~ ('\\y' || esc || '(s|es|ing|ed)?\\y')`,\n   where `esc` escapes every non-alphanumeric so a keyword can never be read as a regex.\n   The optional suffix group is deliberate — it keeps `leaks`, `leaking`, `flooded` and\n   `no heating` firing, because a false negative on a real emergency is far worse than a false\n   positive. Proven 18/19 on a test matrix and re-proven through `sr_gate` itself:\n   gasket/floodlight/fired-up now false, smell-gas/leaking still true.\n   **One judgement call remains, and it is the keyword list not the matcher:** \"we will test the\n   smoke detector\" still reads as an emergency, because `smoke` is genuinely in the message as a\n   whole word. Erring toward emergency there is the safe direction; change it only by editing\n   `emergency_keywords`, never by loosening the matcher.\n2. **`sr_subject_contact` resolves 0.3% of subjects** — 10 of 2,891 `gate.passed` touches. Works\n   for `dm` (80%), returns null for job, lead, contact, voice_call, booking_attempt. The Money Wall\n   now bypasses it with explicit per-type joins. **The gate still uses it** for opt-out on\n   phone-less sends, so roughly one DM in five cannot have opt-out checked by subject.\n3. **`sr_set_tenant_status` does not exist** — the console's master switch has no backend.\n4. **`sms_router` has no row in the `machines` table**, so opt-out events log with a null\n   machine (18 on the tenant). Cosmetic; one insert fixes it.\n5. **Both droplet runners are on UTC, not Toronto** — reactivation fires 06:00 not 10:00, upsell\n   06:15 not 10:15, both inside quiet hours. Fix with `CRON_TZ=America/Toronto` in the crontab.\n\n**The Supabase confirm dialog has TWO variants — this cost time twice now**\n\n`\"Potential issue detected\"` silently cancels the run if you don't click through, and the button\nlabel DIFFERS by operation:\n- schema/DDL and RLS-touching queries -> **\"Run without RLS\"**\n- DELETE / destructive operations     -> **\"Run query\"**\n\nSep 9 lost the rate-card DDL to the first variant. Sep 10 lost a DELETE to the second, and worse,\nthe automation reported success because it saw a leftover result grid from the previous query.\n**Always verify a write landed by re-querying, never by the results pane or a tool saying ok.**\nDeletes also hit FK order: `leads` and `booking_attempts` both reference `contacts`, so a contact\ncannot be removed until both are gone first.\n\n**Key schema facts learned the hard way**\n\n- A `lead` subject_id is a **Twilio Call SID**, not a lead id. Join `leads.call_sid`. Same for\n  `voice_call` → `voice_calls.call_sid`. Joining on `.id` scores zero.\n- `contacts.phone_e164`, not `phone`. `events` has `created_at`, no `occurred_at`.\n- Never verify a write inside the statement that performed it — the sibling sub-select reads the\n  snapshot from statement start.\n- Silence in `events` is NOT evidence of failure. Every machine is input-driven and most inputs\n  stopped when the seed ended Sep 6. A booking must age ~1 hour before it is nudged.\n\n**Where things stand**\n\n- **Owner Console = \"Field Command\" in Lovable.** All six screens built, MODELLED banner, and\n  \"— not live yet\" rendering correctly. A **sign-in screen** was added Day 16 so it reads through\n  an authenticated session rather than `anon`. Auth user is `demo@secondring.test`, role `owner`\n  on the demo tenant.\n- **`demo_tenant_read` policies exist on 8 tables** (scoped to the demo tenant only). These were\n  added as a fallback before the sign-in approach landed. **They are now redundant — consider\n  dropping them** so `anon` has no read path at all.\n- **DM Rescue has never received a real Instagram message, and CANNOT until the app is published.**\n  210 events: 199 seeded, 11 gate probes, zero real inbound ever. Meta's own webhook panel states:\n  **\"To receive webhooks, your app must be in published state.\"** The app is Unpublished. The webhook\n  fields (messages, comments, message_edit, message_reactions) are already subscribed at v26.0 and\n  the bridge is healthy — the plumbing is right, the app simply is not published.\n  **Do not repeat this dead end:** `extinctreps` was added as an Instagram Tester on Day 16 and the\n  invite was accepted, and it changed nothing. An Instagram Tester role lets you CALL the API in\n  development mode; it does NOT enable webhook delivery. Those are different mechanisms. The only\n  route to a real inbound DM is App Review + Business verification, and business verification was\n  already rejected once because the certificate did not show the address. This is weeks of work and\n  Meta's queue, not a build task.\n\n**Machines with live evidence from Day 16:** voice_receptionist, dm_rescue (gate holds),\ncustomer_reactivation, upsell_engine, booking_abandonment (refused its nudge at 07:45 for quiet\nhours, sent it at 08:00:42 — the best single demo artifact you have).\n\n**Still without today's evidence:** `missed_call_rescue`, `speed_to_lead`, `review_engine`,\n`missed_appointment`. All have solid 30-day histories; the demo scripts already stand on them.\n`email_lead_capture` stays off on purpose since Sep 6.\n\nAsk me what I want to work on before starting anything.\n"},{"date":"2026-09-13","group":"work","kind":"guide","label":"Study guide","origin":"original","source":"SecondRing Vault/Day 17/day-17-study-guide.md","title":"Day 17 study guide — the contract day, for real","md":"# Day 17 study guide — the contract day, for real\n\n*Sun Sep 13 → Mon Sep 14, 2026. Plain English. Everything below was proved against the live database (`tjmxqyuzglcfnhgwamit`) or the live site, not the roadmap.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. What today was\n\nDays 15 and 16 built real things (the rate card, the pricing engine, the console views, the voice fix) but left a pile of **promises the platform was making that nothing backed up**. The console had a master switch with no function behind it. The onboarding form documented five SQL functions that did not exist. The gate could only check opt-out on 0.3% of sends. Today closed every one of those, then proved each one from the outside.\n\nThink of it like a house where the electrical panel had labels for breakers that were never wired. Today wired them, then flipped each one and checked the light came on.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The five things that changed, and why they matter on a sales call\n\n**The master switch exists.** `sr_set_tenant_status(tenant, 'paused', reason)` stops every machine on a business instantly. Not \"each machine checks its own switch\" — one row on the business flips, and the gate refuses everything, *including emergencies*, with the reason `tenant_paused`. Turning it back on demands a typed reason, so nobody resumes by accident. The Silence Log shows it as \"Everything is paused — the master switch is off.\"\n\n*Sales line:* \"There is one switch. You hit it, everything stops, and the log shows you hit it.\"\n\n**The notch now knows who you are.** `sr_set_machine_notch` used to be hard-coded to the demo company. Now it takes the business from the signed-in login, refuses another business's machines, and records who flipped it. Same three arguments the console already sends.\n\n**The gate can see through any subject.** Every send carries a \"subject\" (a job id, a lead's call SID, a booking, a review, an approval). The opt-out check could only follow Instagram subjects — 10 of 2,891 gate touches. Now it follows all of them: **99.3%**. Proved live: an opted-out customer got caught through a *job id* with no phone on the send. This is the difference between \"STOP works on texts\" and \"STOP works everywhere.\"\n\n**Tenant #2 went through the form.** `sr_onboard_tenant` now exists, written against the real tables. Grand River Mechanical's profile went through the form's own payload: 9 machines switched, 59 settings written *with Fennwick's floors and ceilings inherited* (a new client's silence timer cannot be installed without its 8–60 second bounds ever again), price list untouched, and the read-back function returned everything the webhook checks. The whole submission is archived in `tenant_onboarding_submissions`, so the sections no machine reads yet (people, payroll, billing) are kept, not lost.\n\n**Ontario's 10% rule is a database rule.** An estimate cannot be configured with an envelope over 10%. A quote that supersedes an estimate cannot leave draft more than 10% above it — the database refuses with a plain-English message that says *issue a change order instead*. A change order the customer agrees to is the only way up. Proved with a rolled-back test: +8% passed, +12% refused, +12% as a change order passed, and a \"sent\" quote with no approval was still refused by the Day 15 guard.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The registry is v10 (and honest)\n\n`onboarding.html` now carries 17 machines and 20 stack categories, audited clean. What changed:\n\n- **Drift fixed.** The form offered a mode called `approval` that the database rejects. The radios are now the four real values, labelled the way the console says them: Let it run / Ask me / Watch / Off. Every old entry carries its database key (`missed_call_rescue`, `speed_to_lead`, `review_engine`, `estimate_ladder`) and the form sends that key.\n- **Settings ids match what the runners read** (`rescue_delay_seconds`, `reminder_hours`, `review_delay_hours`, `happy_rating_threshold`, `max_nudges`, `nudge_days`).\n- **The voice timer default is 15, floor 8, ceiling 60.** The registry still said 6 — the exact number that hung up on callers on Day 16. Number fields now carry min/max.\n- **Three machines that were never in the registry are in:** Booking Abandonment (live), Email Lead Saver (blocked — unpublished since Sep 6, decision still open), and the six Category B machines with quote guardrails as settings.\n- **The Handoff docs name the functions that exist.** The v4 SQL embedded in the page is marked historical with a \"do not run\" note.\n\nDatabase and site were bumped to 10 in the same hour: `sr_current_schema_version()` = 10, `SCHEMA_VERSION = 10`, commit `ee307fc`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Proof you can re-run yourself\n\nOpen the Supabase SQL editor and paste any of these. Each returns a single JSON cell. **Verify a write in a separate statement, never in the one that did it** — that bit us again today (the GR onboarding receipt read the pre-write snapshot).\n\n**Master switch — pause, prove, resume**\n```sql\nselect public.sr_set_tenant_status('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000','paused','proof','me');\nselect public.sr_mode('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000','voice_receptionist');          -- paused\nselect public.sr_check_send('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000','voice_receptionist','+15005550117','contact',null,'smell gas')->>'reason'; -- tenant_paused\nselect public.sr_set_tenant_status('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000','active','proof done','me');\n```\n(Resume with an empty reason and it refuses. Fennwick is back on `active` right now.)\n\n**Opt-out through a job id**\n```sql\nwith c as (select id from contacts where tenant_id='00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000' and opted_out_at is not null limit 1),\n     j as (select id from jobs where contact_id=(select id from c) limit 1)\nselect public.sr_check_send('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000','review_engine',null,'job',(select id::text from j),'how did we do?')->>'reason';  -- contact_opted_out\n```\n\n**How much the gate can see now**\n```sql\nselect round(100.0*sum((public.sr_subject_contact(tenant_id,subject_type,subject_id) is not null)::int)/count(*),1)\nfrom events where tenant_id='00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000' and event_type in ('gate.passed','gate.blocked')\nand subject_type in ('lead','job','contact','voice_call','booking_attempt','review');   -- 99.3\n```\n\n**Tenant #2 read-back**\n```sql\nselect public.sr_tenant_config('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111','review_engine') - 'greeting' - 'hours_text' - 'services' - 'emergency_policy';\nselect * from public.sr_tenants_missing('sms_router');   -- only \"catB: Nocard Heating Ltd\"\n```\n\n**The 10% rule** — run `d17-regression.sql` section \"CPA trigger proof\" from the Day 17 folder; it rolls itself back and prints `est_ok variance15_refused plus8_ok plus12_refused change_order_ok sent_without_approval_refused`.\n\n**Version and posture**\n```sql\nselect sr_current_schema_version();   -- 10\nselect m.key, tm.mode, tm.enabled from tenant_machines tm join machines m on m.id=tm.machine_id\n where tm.tenant_id='00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000' and m.category='A_revenue' order by 1;\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. What still needs you (in the order it hurts)\n\n1. **Droplet clock.** Both runners fire at 6am Toronto inside quiet hours because the box is on UTC. Ubuntu 24.04's crontab(5) man page documents `CRON_TZ` (\"specifies the time zone specific for the cron table\"), so the two-line fix is:\n   ```\n   crontab -l | head -5                       # look first: is the 10:00 / 10:15 line in THIS crontab, or root's (sudo crontab -l)?\n   (echo 'CRON_TZ=America/Toronto'; crontab -l | grep -v '^CRON_TZ=') | crontab - && crontab -l | head -3\n   ```\n   Use `sudo crontab -l` / `sudo crontab -` instead if the runner lines live in root's crontab. Paste the output back. The schedule lines stay `0 10` and `15 10` — they now mean Toronto. (Alternative that also works: `sudo timedatectl set-timezone America/Toronto && sudo systemctl restart cron`, which moves every log timestamp to local time too.)\n2. **Voice timer start.** Paste `grep -n \"silence\" /opt/sr-voice-bridge/*.js | head -40` and I will hand you the one-line change (start the clock after the estimated speech length, not at speech start — ConversationRelay does not tell us when it finished talking).\n3. **n8n sign-in** (the tab is open on the sign-in page, Chrome has the password). Once you are in I import the onboarding webhook workflow, set `ONBOARD_SECRET`, and Grand River goes through the *actual* form end to end — plus I fire ripe inputs at the four machines with no Day 16 evidence (missed call, speed-to-lead, review, missed appointment).\n4. **Solicitor.** Still the hard gate on going paid. Angrove $995 / Pawlina $300 / Muddasir flat-fee quote — pick one and book.\n5. **Two one-word decisions:** delete the synthetic tenant `catB: Nocard Heating Ltd` (0 rows attached, left over from Sep 9)? Flip `email_lead_capture` on Fennwick to Off so the console tile stops reading \"Let it run\" for a machine that is unpublished?\n\n---\n\n## 6. Lessons worth keeping\n\n- **Never verify inside the statement that wrote.** A CTE that calls a writing function and sibling sub-selects that \"check\" it will read the old snapshot and tell you nothing happened. Re-query in a second statement. Today's GR receipt said `tm: null, cfg_n: 0` and was wrong.\n- **The form is the contract; the database is the truth.** The registry said `approval`; the database only accepts `approval_required`. The form said the silence default was 6; the database was fixed to 15 on Day 16. Registry v10 closes both, and the form now sends the database's own keys.\n- **Fennwick is the schema of record for settings.** When a new client gets a setting, its floor/ceiling/description come from Fennwick's row. If Fennwick's row has no bounds, neither will the client's — so give Fennwick's row bounds first.\n- **A guardrail is a database rule or it is a habit.** \"Approval required before sending\" is a CHECK. \"Ten percent over an estimate\" is a trigger. The runner can be wrong; the table cannot.\n- **The Supabase dialog has three variants now:** \"Run query\" (destructive), \"Run without RLS\" (creates a table — including temp tables and, oddly, function bodies that mention one), and no dialog at all for plain CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION. Always re-query.\n"},{"date":"2026-09-13","group":"work","kind":"findings","label":"Findings","origin":"original","source":"SecondRing Vault/Day 17/day-17-findings.md","title":"Day 17 — Sun Sep 13 → Mon Sep 14, 2026 — findings","md":"# Day 17 — Sun Sep 13 → Mon Sep 14, 2026 — findings\n\nEverything below was read off the live Supabase database (`tjmxqyuzglcfnhgwamit`), the live site, or a\nreceipt from a statement that ran. Nothing comes from the roadmap, the registry, or memory.\nSession ran 02:05 → 03:15 UTC (22:05 → 23:15 Toronto, Sunday night).\n\n---\n\n## State at session start (verified, not assumed)\n\n- `sr_current_schema_version()` = 9; 37 machines; registry SCHEMA_VERSION 9 with 9 entries.\n- Five `sr_*` functions the form documents did **not** exist: `sr_set_tenant_status`,\n  `sr_onboard_tenant`, `sr_tenant_config`, `sr_send_log`, `sr_tenants_missing`.\n- `sr_set_machine_notch(_machine_id, _enabled, _mode)` existed but was **hard-coded to the demo\n  tenant** with actor `anon_console`.\n- `sr_subject_contact` only understood `ig:<id>` / `contact:<uuid>` — and ignored `p_subject_type`\n  entirely. It resolved 10 of 2,891 gate touches.\n- `sms_router` had no `machines` row; 18 events on the tenant carried a null machine.\n- `pg_cron` job 1 (`sr-expire-approvals-hourly`) running and succeeding every hour; 10 pending\n  approvals, 0 past expiry.\n- **Grand River Mechanical already existed** as tenant `11111111-…` (created Sep 9 with a 46-service\n  rate card via `sr_onboard_rate_card`) but had 0 `tenant_machines`, 0 configs, 0 contacts.\n- A third tenant, **`catB: Nocard Heating Ltd`** (`22222222-…`, created Sep 9 16:42 UTC, 0 rows\n  attached) — a synthetic leftover from the rate-card conformance work. Still there; delete is\n  Ahmed's call.\n- `demo_tenant_read` anon SELECT policies on 8 tables. Only one auth user exists:\n  `fc7c6b77…` = `demo@secondring.test` (this is the same account the skill calls \"Ahmed's auth\n  user\"), owner on Fennwick in `tenant_users`.\n- The n8n webhook `POST /webhook/onboard-tenant` is **not registered** (404 from n8n) — the\n  onboarding workflow embedded in the form was never imported. `/dm/health` healthy, observe.\n- The registry's voice default `silence_reprompt_seconds` was still **6** — the Day 16 hang-up value.\n- The form's mode radios were hard-coded `autonomous / approval / observe / paused` — `approval` is\n  not a value the DB accepts, so `customer_reactivation`'s `approval_required` default matched no\n  radio and the form sent `observe`.\n- `v_console_silence` mapped `paused` / `not_installed` in its plain-English CASE, but the gate\n  writes `machine_paused` / `machine_not_installed` — those rows rendered raw.\n- Repo commit `1b1e8fa` (Sep 9 12:31, \"trade service templates + rate-card intake\") was not in the\n  skill's history ledger. It added `RATE_CARD_TEMPLATES` + \"Load standard list\" to the form.\n\n## Shipped, in order, each verified by a separate re-query\n\n1. **`sr_subject_contact`** rewritten (plpgsql, `sr_subject_contact_core` with a depth guard).\n   Resolves prefixed channel ids, `contact:<uuid|phone>`, bare uuids across contacts / jobs /\n   booking_attempts / reviews / leads / voice_calls / **approvals (one hop to the approval's own\n   subject, then its payload's contact_id / phone)**, Twilio Call SIDs via `leads.call_sid` and\n   `voice_calls.call_sid` (with phone fallbacks), and bare phone numbers.\n   **Result on Fennwick's gate touches: lead 1100/1101, job 985/985, contact 664/682, voice_call\n   226/226, booking_attempt 76/78, dm 8/17, review 3/3 — 99.3% overall (was 0.3%).**\n2. **`sms_router`** machines row (`A_revenue`) + Fennwick `tenant_machines` row (autonomous).\n3. **`sr_set_tenant_status(p_tenant, p_status, p_reason, p_actor)`** — SECURITY DEFINER; signed-in\n   users limited to their own tenants; `active|paused|churned`; resume needs a reason ≥ 3 chars;\n   logs `tenant.paused` / `tenant.resumed` / `tenant.churned` (severity warn, machine null).\n4. **`sr_mode`** returns `paused` for every machine when the tenant is paused/churned.\n5. **`sr_check_send`** gained check 1b: tenant paused → `tenant_paused` (after opt-out, before\n   machine mode). Applied as an in-place edit of the live definition (fetched, string-inserted,\n   re-created) — no retyping.\n6. **`sr_set_machine_notch(_machine_id, _enabled, _mode, _tenant_id default null)`** — old 3-arg\n   function dropped; tenant from `tenant_users` for a signed-in user (explicit `_tenant_id` must be\n   one of theirs; more than one tenant with none named → error), demo tenant for service role;\n   `observe_only` normalised to `observe`; actor recorded. Revoked from anon.\n7. **`sr_tenants_missing(p_machine_key)`** — tenants (not churned) without that machine; raises on\n   an unknown key.\n8. **`sr_tenant_config(p_tenant_id, p_machine_key)`** — `sr_machine_profile` + tenant-level\n   configs + `machine{key,name,category,enabled,mode,installed_at,effective_mode}` + `sms{send_window_start,\n   send_window_end,quiet_hours,max_per_contact_per_day}` + `is_demo` + `schema_version`.\n9. **`v_console_silence`** re-created (still `security_invoker`) with both the gate's and the seed's\n   reason keys, plus `tenant_paused` → \"Everything is paused — the master switch is off\".\n10. **`sr_onboard_tenant(p_payload)`** — the form endpoint, against the live schema. Full or patch\n    mode. Writes `tenants`, `tenant_machines`, `machine_configs` (via `sr_set_config`, which inherits\n    value_type / min / max / description from Fennwick's row for the same machine+key — `sr_config_meta`),\n    tenant-level knobs (`quiet_hours` from the send window, `max_messages_per_contact_per_day`,\n    `max_autonomous_value_cents`, `human_approval_above_cents`, `max_discount_pct`,\n    `photo_to_quote.max_variance_pct` clamped to 10), `contacts` suppression seed (+ `contact.opted_out`\n    events on `sms_router`), the price list through `sr_onboard_rate_card` when any service carries a\n    price (a refused card is a warning, not a failure), the master switch last, and the whole payload\n    into the new `tenant_onboarding_submissions` table (RLS, tenant-scoped select). Registry keys are\n    mapped by `sr_registry_machine_key`; `approval`/`observe_only`/`watch`/`ask_me`/`let_it_run`/`off`\n    are normalised; config ids `text_delay_sec → rescue_delay_seconds`, `reminder_delay_hours →\n    reminder_hours`, `nudge_stages → max_nudges`; comma strings become arrays for the voice list keys\n    and `estimate_ladder.nudge_days`. Service role only.\n11. **Ontario 10%**: `quotes_estimate_variance_cpa` CHECK (`kind <> 'estimate' or max_variance_pct\n    <= 10`) + `trg_quotes_cpa_guard` (before insert/update of status, total_cents,\n    supersedes_quote_id, kind): a non-draft, non-change-order quote superseding an estimate may not\n    exceed `floor(estimate.total_cents * 1.10)`. Message names both quote numbers and the limit and\n    says to issue a change order.\n12. **`demo_tenant_read` dropped on all 8 tables.** `tenants` now has only `tenants_select` /\n    `tenants_update` for `authenticated`.\n13. **Tenant #2 through the form's own payload.** The live `onboarding.html` was filled with the\n    Grand River v2 profile in Chrome, `collect()` produced the payload (patch mode: owner, channels,\n    pricing_rules, sms, send_rules, review, guardrails, machines, ops — business and services left\n    alone so the trade string and the 46-service card survive), one edit (voice silence 6 → 15), then\n    `sr_onboard_tenant` ran it. **Re-queried in a separate statement:** 9 tenant_machines\n    (missed_call_rescue autonomous on · sms_router autonomous on · speed_to_lead approval_required on\n    · review_engine observe on · five off), 59 configs, tenant-level quiet_hours 20:00–08:00, cap 3,\n    max_discount 5, human_approval_above 1,000,000¢, max_autonomous 50,000¢; owner/phones/links\n    written; rate card untouched (46 items); 1 submission archived; `sr_tenant_config` read-back\n    complete; `sr_tenants_missing('sms_router')` now lists only catB. GR's config rows then\n    inherited Fennwick's bounds (silence 15 with 8/60; 13 rows bounded).\n    Placeholder numbers, deliberately inert: owner `+15005550118`, Twilio `+15005550100`.\n14. **Registry v10** — commit `ee307fc` (18 exact-anchor edits, byte-identical to the headless-tested\n    file, SHA `51fd46d4…`): SCHEMA_VERSION 10; `dbKey` on the four legacy entries and `collect()`\n    emits `machine_key = dbKey || key` (+ `registry_key`); `build2_lead_triage` default\n    `approval_required`; mode radios = the four DB values labelled Let it run / Ask me / Watch / Off;\n    config ids aligned to DB keys; voice silence default 15 with min 8 / max 60 and number fields\n    render min/max; `booking_abandonment` (live, `website_booking`), `email_lead_capture` (blocked)\n    and the six Category B entries with quote guardrails as config; six new STACK categories; the\n    runtime-contract table names `sr_gate` / `sr_check_send` / `sr_tenant_config` /\n    `sr_set_tenant_status` / `sr_onboard_tenant`; a \"do not run the embedded v4 SQL\" note.\n    Audit: 17 machines, 20 categories, no dupes, no competes collisions, no orphan categories.\n    Live-verified in Ahmed's Chrome after deploy.\n15. **`sr_current_schema_version()` = 10** (same hour as the commit) and a `supplier_negotiation`\n    machines row (`B_sales`, v0.1) so registry ⊆ machines. 39 machines now.\n16. **Roadmap** — commit `24d0198`: Day 17 card appended to Category A (keys `bA-66..69`, nothing\n    shifts), Category B Day 1 rewritten to what is true, one-time seed `lab.roadmap.seed.day17`\n    ticks `bA-66..69` and `bB-1..3` (the solicitor box `bB-0` stays open). Live-verified: seed\n    applied, 17 A days, B Day 1 title updated.\n\n## Proof (regression, 36/36, `d17-regression.sql`)\n\nSubject resolution counts as above · opt-out caught through a job id · clean contact + emergency\nthrough a job id → `ok` · emergency word boundary (gasket/floodlight false, smell gas/leaking true) ·\nquiet hours true at 22:43 → plain text `quiet_hours` · master switch: pause ok, every `sr_mode`\n`paused`, gate `tenant_paused` even on an emergency, resume without reason refused, resume ok,\n`tenant.paused,tenant.resumed` logged, status back to `active` · notch: Watch → `observe_only`,\nrestore, 2 `notch_changed` events with actor `service_role`, bogus mode refused · approvals: expire\nsweep 1, pending → `needs_approval`, approved → `ok` (emergency to clear quiet hours),\n`approval.approved` audited, rows deleted · Silence Log plain rows for `quiet_hours` and\n`tenant_paused` · `sr_tenants_missing` + `sr_tenant_config` shapes · posture restored.\n\n**CPA proof (rolled back inside a DO block):** `est_ok variance15_refused plus8_ok plus12_refused\nchange_order_ok sent_without_approval_refused`. `quotes` count is still 0.\n\n## Side effects to know about\n\n- ~13 new events on Fennwick (2 `tenant.paused`, 2 `tenant.resumed`, 2 `notch_changed`,\n  2 `gate.blocked` with subjects `day17:silence` / `day17:master`, 2 `approval.requested`,\n  1 `approval.expired`, 1 `approval.approved`) — append-only, permanent, all tagged `day17`.\n- Grand River: `tenant.updated`, `rate_card.*` untouched from Sep 9.\n- The form's embedded n8n workflow JSON still references `sr_log_send` / `sr_can_send` in the\n  gate sub-workflow section; the onboarding workflow itself calls `rpc/sr_onboard_tenant` and\n  `rpc/sr_tenant_config` with the parameter names that now exist.\n\n## Still open after today\n\n1. **Droplet**: `CRON_TZ=America/Toronto` at the top of the runner crontab (Ubuntu 24.04 crontab(5)\n   documents it) — or `timedatectl`. Ahmed at the DO console.\n2. **Voice timer** starts at speech start; needs the bridge source (`grep -n silence\n   /opt/sr-voice-bridge/*.js`). ConversationRelay gives no \"finished speaking\" event — the fix is an\n   estimated-duration delay before arming the silence timer.\n3. **n8n**: import the onboarding webhook workflow, set `ONBOARD_SECRET`, run Grand River through the\n   actual form URL. Then give ripe inputs to `missed_call_rescue`, `speed_to_lead`, `review_engine`,\n   `missed_appointment` (n8n sign-in is Ahmed's).\n4. **Decisions**: delete `catB: Nocard Heating Ltd`? flip `email_lead_capture` on Fennwick to Off\n   (it is `enabled/autonomous` in `tenant_machines` while the n8n workflow is unpublished — the console\n   tile would say \"Let it run\")? Money Wall attribution rule. Email Lead Saver retire vs re-home.\n5. **Solicitor** — the hard gate on paid Category B.\n6. Registry ids not yet aligned for `speed_to_lead` (`alert_on`, `hot_keywords`, `dedupe_window_hours`,\n   `auto_reply` vs DB `hot_lead_owner_alert`, `junk_silent_log`, `response_delay_seconds`) and\n   `upsell_engine.min_confidence` (form 0.7 vs DB int) — written as-is, harmless, untidy.\n7. `console_owner` still has no registry entry (it is not a machine; decide whether it should be).\n8. Watchdog logged nothing in 10+ days — still unconfirmed whether it watches anything.\n"},{"date":"2026-09-13","group":"work","kind":"startup","label":"Startup prompt (Day 18)","origin":"original","source":"SecondRing Vault/Day 17/day-18-startup-prompt.md","title":"Startup prompt — next session (after Day 17, Sun Sep 13 → Mon Sep 14 2026)","md":"# Startup prompt — next session (after Day 17, Sun Sep 13 → Mon Sep 14 2026)\n\nPaste this at the start of the next session.\n\n---\n\nLoad the `ai-trade-business-os` skill first, then read `claude/day-17-findings.md` in the project.\nDay 17 is done. Every Day 15/16 carry-over that lives in the database is closed and was re-queried\nafter the fact; the site carries registry v10 and the Day 17 roadmap card. Summary of what is now\ntrue (verified live, not reported):\n\n**Live in the database (schema 10, 39 machines, 30 tables)**\n- Master switch: `sr_set_tenant_status(tenant, status, reason, actor)`. Pause → every `sr_mode`\n  reads `paused`, the gate refuses everything with `tenant_paused` (emergencies too), resume needs a\n  typed reason, events `tenant.paused` / `tenant.resumed`. Silence Log row: \"Everything is paused —\n  the master switch is off\".\n- Notch: `sr_set_machine_notch(_machine_id, _enabled, _mode, _tenant_id default null)` — tenant\n  from the signed-in user's `tenant_users`, actor recorded, no anon.\n- `sr_subject_contact` resolves 99.3% of gate touches (job, lead call SID, voice call, booking,\n  review, approval one-hop, contact uuid, phone, ig:). Opt-out now catches phone-less sends.\n- Form contract: `sr_onboard_tenant(p_payload)`, `sr_tenant_config(p_tenant_id, p_machine_key)`,\n  `sr_tenants_missing(p_machine_key)`, helper `sr_set_config` (inherits Fennwick's\n  value_type/min/max/description for new rows — Fennwick is the schema of record), archive table\n  `tenant_onboarding_submissions`. `sr_send_log` / `sr_can_send` / `sr_log_send` were never built and\n  are marked historical on the page — the gate is `sr_gate` → `sr_check_send`.\n- Ontario 10%: CHECK `quotes_estimate_variance_cpa` + trigger `trg_quotes_cpa_guard`\n  (change orders exempt, drafts exempt). Proven with a rolled-back matrix; `quotes` still has 0 rows.\n- `demo_tenant_read` gone from all 8 tables; the console must read as `demo@secondring.test`\n  (`fc7c6b77…`, owner on Fennwick).\n- `sms_router` and `supplier_negotiation` have machines rows.\n- Tenant #2 = Grand River Mechanical (`11111111-…`, `is_demo`, inert numbers `+15005550118` owner /\n  `+15005550100` Twilio): 9 tenant_machines, 59 configs with bounds, 46-service rate card from Sep 9,\n  1 archived submission. It went through the form's own `collect()` payload, not the n8n webhook.\n- Synthetic tenant `catB: Nocard Heating Ltd` (`22222222-…`, 0 rows attached) still exists.\n\n**Site (repo `blondedca-wq/5-core-systems`, both live-verified)**\n- `ee307fc` — registry v10: `dbKey` on legacy entries, `collect()` sends canonical `machine_key`,\n  radios = `autonomous / approval_required / observe / paused` labelled Let it run / Ask me / Watch /\n  Off, config ids aligned to DB keys, voice silence 15 (8–60), `booking_abandonment`,\n  `email_lead_capture` (blocked), six Category B machines, six new STACK categories, runtime-contract\n  docs corrected, \"do not run the embedded v4 SQL\" note. 17 machines, 20 categories.\n- `24d0198` — roadmap Day 17 card (`bA-66..69`), Category B Day 1 rewritten, seed\n  `lab.roadmap.seed.day17` ticks `bA-66..69` + `bB-1..3`; `bB-0` (solicitor) open.\n\n**Update — Sep 15 session (continuation of Day 17)**\n- ✅ `email_lead_capture` flipped **Off** on Fennwick (`sr_set_machine_notch`, enabled=false / mode=paused; verified). Tile now reads Off.\n- 🅿️ Nocard delete **staged** in the Supabase SQL editor (guarded DELETE; 0 rows attached, verified across all 29 tenant-scoped tables). Ahmed clicks Run → \"Run query\". Not yet deleted.\n- ✅ Voice-bridge silence-timer fix — Ahmed reports it was **done in a previous session**. Removed from the list.\n- 📄 New: `lawful-business-guide.md` (project doc `claude/day-17-lawful-business-guide.md` + vault `Day 17/`) — CASL / CRTC Do-Not-Call / PIPEDA / Ontario CPA / call-recording, mapped to the machines, with a build checklist and solicitor questions. Plan: finish builds, Ahmed gets a solicitor to review after, compliance research runs in parallel.\n- 🔧 Onboarding webhook workflow **rewired and ready to import** (`/home/claude/day17/onboard-workflow.placeholder.json`): the two Supabase HTTP nodes now use the stored `Supabase account` credential (id `gcZVZpIyZNc0SDgL`, type `supabaseApi`) with hardcoded RPC URLs — no `$env` dependency; the Auth gate carries a `__ONBOARD_SECRET__` placeholder to replace with a real secret at import (avoids a droplet restart). Import via clipboard paste into a new n8n workflow.\n\n**Needs Ahmed at the keyboard (in order)**\n1. Droplet crontab: `CRON_TZ=America/Toronto` at the top (Ubuntu 24.04 crontab(5) documents it) —\n   check whether the 10:00/10:15 runner lines are in the ubuntu or root crontab first.\n2. n8n sign-in (session keeps dropping on reconnect). Then: import the rewired onboarding workflow\n   (placeholder JSON above), set the real secret in the Auth gate, submit Grand River through the\n   real URL, and fire ripe inputs at `missed_call_rescue`, `speed_to_lead`, `review_engine`,\n   `missed_appointment`.\n3. Run the staged Nocard DELETE (one click).\n4. Solicitor review of the customer agreement + DPA + estimate template (hand them\n   `lawful-business-guide.md` §8 and the existing `b1-solicitor-brief.md`) — after builds, per Ahmed.\n\n**Transport notes that saved time today**\n- Supabase SQL editor: `window.monaco.editor.getEditors()[0].setValue(sql)`; click Run; read\n  `document.querySelector('[role=\"gridcell\"]').textContent` — return ONE `json_build_object(...)::text`\n  cell and read it in 900-char slices (the JS tool truncates ~1000 chars). Strings containing `=`\n  and `;` trip the tool's cookie filter — replace them before returning.\n- Three dialog variants: \"Run query\" (DELETE/DROP), \"Run without RLS\" (any CREATE TABLE incl. temp\n  tables and function bodies that mention one), none for CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION. Use `find` for\n  the button; the window size changes.\n- Edit a live function without retyping: `select pg_get_functiondef('public.fn(args)'::regprocedure)`,\n  string-replace in JS, `setValue`, Run, then `like '%marker%'` receipt.\n- **Never verify inside the statement that wrote** — the GR onboarding receipt read the old snapshot.\n- GitHub web editor: `document.querySelector('.cm-content').cmTile.view`; apply exact-anchor\n  `(old,new)` edits via `view.dispatch`, compare SHA-256 with the locally rendered file, then the\n  commit dialog needs the \"Commit changes...\" button clicked by coordinate (the first ref click did\n  not open it). The sandbox cannot push (proxy denies the repo).\n- The sandbox's Chromium cannot reach github.io through the proxy; curl can. Verify live pages in\n  Ahmed's Chrome.\n\nCategory B Day 2 (Photo → Quote I — vision pipeline) is next on the roadmap: photo intake to\nSupabase Storage, scope extraction (`b2-scope-contract.md`), ten seeded photo sets. Ahmed still owes\nthe 10 photo sets from work and the PII-scrubbed blueprint.\n\nAsk me what I want to work on before starting anything.\n"},{"date":"2026-09-13","group":"work","kind":"schema","label":"Live schema notes","origin":"original","source":"SecondRing Vault/Day 17/schema-notes.md","title":"Live schema (read Sep 14 02:20 UTC from tjmxqyuzglcfnhgwamit)","md":"# Live schema (read Sep 14 02:20 UTC from tjmxqyuzglcfnhgwamit)\napprovals: id, tenant_id, machine_id, action_type, subject_type, subject_id, summary, proposed_payload jsonb, reason, expires_at, status, decided_by, decided_at, decision_note, edited_payload jsonb, created_at\nbooking_attempts: id, tenant_id, contact_id, name, phone_e164, service, source, status, nudge_stage int, last_activity_at, last_nudge_at, replied_at, created_at, updated_at\ncontact_identities: id, tenant_id, contact_id, channel, external_id, created_at\ncontacts: id, tenant_id, name, phone_e164, email, consent_sms bool, consent_sms_at, opted_out_at, source, notes, first_seen_at, last_contact_at, created_at, updated_at\nevents: id bigint, tenant_id, machine_id, event_type, severity, subject_type, subject_id, summary, payload jsonb, ai_model, ai_confidence, ai_reasoning, autonomous bool, guardrail_key, guardrail_pass bool, created_at\njobs: id, tenant_id, contact_id, description, address, status, scheduled_for, completed_at, value_cents bigint, created_at, updated_at\nleads: id, tenant_id, machine_id, contact_id, created_at, updated_at, caller_number, call_sid, status, dial_status, after_hours bool, first_sms_at, last_inbound_at, followups_sent int, replied bool, opted_out bool, ai_job_type, ai_urgency, ai_summary, suggested_reply, message, notes, lead_id\nmachine_configs: id, tenant_id, machine_id, key, value jsonb, value_type, min_value, max_value, description, requires_provider_approval bool, updated_by, updated_at, created_at\nmachines: id, key, name, category, description, version, created_at   (NO status column)\nquote_lines: id, tenant_id, quote_id, line_no, kind, rate_card_item_id, material_id, labour_rate_id, premium_id, tier_id, description, qty, unit, unit_cents, line_cents, taxable, ai_confidence, ai_reasoning, evidence_ids[], variance_flag, created_at, updated_at\nquote_log: id, tenant_id, quote_id text, customer_name, phone, quote_amount text, job_description, status, nudge_stage, sent_at, last_nudge_at, replied_at, logged_at   (NEVER ALTER)\nquotes: id, tenant_id, quote_no, version, supersedes_quote_id, kind, status, source, contact_id, job_id, rate_card_id, tier_no, premium_codes[], currency, subtotal_cents, discount_cents, tax_label, tax_rate_pct, tax_cents, total_cents, deposit_pct, deposit_cents, max_variance_pct, variance_flags jsonb, ai_model, ai_confidence, ai_reasoning, scope_text, exclusions_text, notes_internal, terms_version, site_address, valid_until date, approval_mode, approval_id, approved_by, approved_at, sent_at, viewed_at, accepted_at, declined_at, expired_at, ladder_synced_at, created_by, created_at, updated_at\nrate_card_items: id, tenant_id, rate_card_id, code, name, category, aliases[], unit, pricing_model, base_price_cents, low_price_cents, high_price_cents, labour_hours_est, labour_rate_code, materials_est_cents, materials_markup_pct, min_price_cents, taxable, emergency_eligible, requires_site_visit, ai_quotable, description, notes, sort, active, source, created_at, updated_at\nrate_cards: id, tenant_id, name, currency, tax_label, tax_rate_pct, prices_include_tax, tax_number, materials_markup_pct, min_charge_cents, rounding_cents, effective_from date, effective_to date, status, notes, created_by, created_at, updated_at\nreviews: id, tenant_id, job_id, contact_id, phone_e164, status, rating, comment, asked_at, reminded_at, replied_at, created_at, updated_at\ntenant_machines: id, tenant_id, machine_id, enabled bool, mode text, installed_at, updated_at\ntenants: id, business_name, trade, owner_name, owner_phone, owner_email, twilio_number, timezone, booking_link, brand_color, logo_url, plan, status, is_demo, created_at, updated_at, review_link\nvoice_calls: id, tenant_id, call_sid, from_number, to_number, contact_id, transcript jsonb, turns, caller_name, service, urgency, grade, summary, outcome, escalated, escalate_reason, started_at, ended_at, duration_sec   (NO created_at)\n\nTransport: monaco setValue(sql) -> click Run (ref \"Run\" button) -> read document.querySelector('[role=\"gridcell\"]').textContent (single json_agg cell; JS tool output truncates ~1000 chars so read in slices via window.__r)\n"},{"date":"2026-09-14","group":"work","kind":"law","label":"Lawful-business guide","origin":"original","source":"SecondRing Vault/Day 17/lawful-business-guide.md","title":"Staying on the right side of the law — SecondRing, plain English","md":"# Staying on the right side of the law — SecondRing, plain English\n\n*Written Sep 15, 2026. Current as of today. This is research to make the build compliant by design — it is **not legal advice**, and every \"must\" below is the kind of thing the solicitor confirms before we go paid. Where a law is mid-change, I've flagged it as a watch item rather than guessed.*\n\n---\n\n## The short version\n\nFive bodies of law touch what our machines do. Four are federal, one is Ontario:\n\n1. **CASL** — the anti-spam law. Governs every text and email we send on a client's behalf.\n2. **CRTC Unsolicited Telecommunications Rules** — the Do-Not-Call rulebook. Governs phone calls (and some texts), calling hours, and robocalls.\n3. **PIPEDA** — the federal privacy law. Governs the phone numbers, names, addresses, and **photos** we collect and store.\n4. **Ontario Consumer Protection Act** — governs estimates, quotes, and the customer agreement. The 10% rule lives here.\n5. **The Criminal Code (call recording)** — one line, but it decides how the AI voice line has to behave.\n\nThe good news: the send gate we already built is, in legal terms, most of a compliance engine. Opt-out, quiet hours, daily caps, and approval-before-send map almost one-for-one onto what these laws require. This guide names the gaps and the paperwork.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Texting — CASL and the CRTC\n\n**What CASL covers.** CASL applies to any *commercial electronic message* (CEM) — and a text message counts. A CEM is any message that encourages participation in a commercial activity: an offer, a promotion, a \"book now,\" a review ask that points at your business. Three things are required in **every** CEM:\n\n- **Consent** — you need the customer's permission before you send (express or implied — see below).\n- **Identification** — the message says who it's from (the business's name and a way to reach them).\n- **Unsubscribe** — a working, no-cost way to stop, honoured within 10 business days. Our \"reply STOP\" is exactly this.\n\nPenalties are real: up to **$10 million per violation** for a business. That's the number that makes this non-optional.\n\n**Express vs. implied consent — the distinction that matters for us.**\n\n- *Express consent* is an active opt-in (a ticked box that wasn't pre-ticked, a \"yes text me\" on a form). It doesn't expire, and you must keep the record of when/how you got it.\n- *Implied consent* comes from an **existing business relationship** and is **time-limited**:\n  - Someone **bought from / hired** the business → implied consent for **2 years** from that transaction.\n  - Someone **made an inquiry** (called, filled a form, asked a question) → implied consent for **6 months** from the inquiry.\n\nThis is the whole ballgame for our texting machines. Here's the map:\n\n| Machine | Who it texts | Consent basis | Risk | What the message must carry |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **missed_call_rescue** | Someone who just called the business | They initiated contact → inquiry (fresh) | Low | Identify the business; keep it a service reply (\"sorry we missed you\"), not a pitch; STOP line |\n| **speed_to_lead** | A web lead who left their number | Inquiry, 6-month window, and they asked to be contacted | Low | Identify; STOP |\n| **review_engine** | A past customer after a completed job | Transaction, 2-year window | Medium (a review ask can read as commercial) | Identify; STOP; don't chase past opt-out |\n| **reactivation / upsell** | Past customers, sometimes long ago | Transaction, **2-year window** — this is where it expires | **Highest** | Must check the last-job date; if older than 2 years and no express opt-in, don't send |\n\nThe practical rule: **missed-call and speed-to-lead are safe by nature** (the customer just reached out). **Reactivation and upsell are the ones that can drift out of consent** because they reach backward in time — the gate should refuse a send to a contact whose only basis is a transaction older than two years.\n\n**A \"service\" text vs. a \"commercial\" text.** A purely transactional message — \"your appointment is confirmed for Tuesday 2pm\" — is *not* a CEM and doesn't need the consent/unsubscribe machinery. The moment it adds a promotion (\"…and 10% off your next service\"), it becomes a CEM. Keep confirmations clean and keep offers in clearly-consented messages.\n\n**The CRTC layer (Do-Not-Call).** Separate from CASL, the CRTC's Unsolicited Telecommunications Rules add duties that apply to telemarketing calls and can reach promotional texts:\n\n- **Register with the National Do Not Call List (DNCL)** — a business must register *before* making unsolicited calls, **even if its calls are exempt.** This is a concrete to-do: register the operating business.\n- **Scrub against a fresh DNCL** — the list you check against must be **no more than 31 days old** for the area codes you're calling.\n- **Keep an internal Do-Not-Call list** — anyone who says \"stop\" goes on it within **14 days**, and stays for **3 years and 14 days.** Our opt-out table *is* this list — it just has to retain records that long.\n- **Calling hours** — **9:00 a.m.–9:30 p.m. weekdays, 10:00 a.m.–6:00 p.m. weekends** (local time). Our quiet-hours setting should never allow sends outside this outer envelope, whatever a client sets.\n- **Identify yourself** on every call and give contact info on request.\n\n**Existing-business-relationship exemption** exists here too, but note: it exempts you from *the DNCL check*, not from registering, not from the internal list, and not from calling hours.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The phone line — AI voice, robocalls, and recording\n\n**Inbound is easy, outbound is hard.** Our voice receptionist answers calls the customer places. Because the customer initiated the call, the Do-Not-Call and robocall rules mostly don't bite. If we ever build **outbound** AI voice (the machine calls the customer), three heavy rules land at once:\n\n- **ADAD / robocall rule** — an automatic dialing-announcing device (which an outbound AI caller is) requires **prior express consent.** Implied consent is not enough for robocalls. This is a hard gate: no outbound AI voice without a real opt-in on file.\n- DNCL scrub + calling hours, as above.\n- Clear identification at the top of the call.\n\n**Recommendation: keep AI voice inbound-only until the solicitor signs off outbound**, and make \"outbound AI call\" a capability the platform refuses by default.\n\n**Recording and \"am I talking to a robot?\"** Canada is a **one-party-consent** country for recording (Criminal Code s.184) — the business is a party to its own calls, so it may record them. But two things are still expected and protect us:\n\n- **Disclose the recording** at the start (\"this call may be recorded\").\n- **Disclose that it's an automated/AI system.** Passing an AI off as a human can be a deceptive practice under consumer-protection law, and disclosure is quickly becoming the baseline norm for AI voice. A one-line greeting handles both: *\"You've reached [business], this is an automated assistant and the call may be recorded.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 3. Customer data and photos — PIPEDA\n\nPIPEDA is the federal privacy law, and because Ontario has no substantially-similar private-sector law of its own, **it applies to us here.** It's built on a handful of principles that turn into concrete duties:\n\n- **Meaningful consent** to collect, use, or disclose personal information — and phone numbers, names, service addresses, and **photos of a customer's property** are all personal information.\n- **Identify the purpose** up front and **collect only what you need** for it.\n- **Safeguard it** appropriately (access control, encryption in transit and at rest).\n- **Retain only as long as needed**, then dispose of it.\n- **Access and correction** — a customer can ask what you hold and have it fixed.\n- **Accountability** — name someone responsible, and publish a privacy policy. (We already have `privacy-policy.html` and `data-deletion.html` — those are the customer-facing half of this.)\n\n**Two specifics that hit the Photo→Quote machine directly:**\n\n1. **Photos carry more than the job.** A shot of a driveway can include a face, a licence plate, a house number, or mail on a table — all personal information about people who never agreed to anything. This is *why* the intake rule is \"no faces, plates, addresses, paperwork,\" and why resizing/scrubbing before storage is a privacy control, not just a cost saving. Collect the pipe, not the person.\n2. **Where the data lives.** Our storage (Supabase) sits on servers whose region we should confirm and disclose. PIPEDA lets you store or process data outside Canada, but you must **tell customers** it may be handled outside the country and you **stay accountable** for it wherever it sits. One line in the privacy policy covers it; the solicitor confirms the wording.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Pricing and estimates — Ontario Consumer Protection Act\n\n**What's in force today.** The operative law is the **Consumer Protection Act, 2002.** Its estimate rule — a supplier can't charge **more than 10% above a written estimate** without the customer's agreement — is exactly what we've already made a database rule (the CHECK and trigger on `quotes`). A change order the customer agrees to is the only way above the ceiling. That part is built and proven.\n\n**What's coming (a watch item, not a today problem).** Ontario passed a **new Consumer Protection Act, 2023** (Bill 142) — royal assent **Dec 6, 2023** — but as of today it is **still not proclaimed in force**, and its regulations are being drafted through a phased consultation with \"no determined effective date.\" So it changes nothing yet. When it does land, the parts that will touch us are:\n\n- **Express consent to amend or renew** a consumer agreement (no silent changes).\n- **Clearer contract disclosure** — fixed vs. open-ended term, total price, how prices can escalate, all before signing.\n- **Stronger cancellation / cooling-off rights** if the disclosure steps aren't followed exactly.\n\nNone of this blocks Category B. It means the customer-agreement template should be written so those additions are a small edit later, not a rebuild — which is a good question to hand the solicitor now.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Who's actually on the hook — us vs. the client\n\nThis matters because it decides what goes in the client agreement.\n\nThe trade business is the one with the customer relationship — legally, they're usually \"the person on whose behalf\" the messages go out and the one who \"controls\" the customer's personal information. **But CASL and PIPEDA both reach the party who *sends* or *processes* on someone's behalf**, and CASL has vicarious liability — so \"the client told me to\" is not a shield. In plain terms: **we can't outsource our way out of it, and neither can they.** The build has to make the compliant path the only path.\n\nThat splits cleanly into two documents the solicitor should bless:\n\n- **The client (service) agreement** — the client **warrants** they have the right to contact the customers they load in, agrees to the acceptable-use rules, and acknowledges the platform enforces consent/opt-out/hours. This is also where the no-risk guarantee and the liability wording live.\n- **A data-processing addendum (DPA)** — we act as the client's service provider for personal information: what we collect, why, how it's secured, where it's stored, how long we keep it, and that we delete on request. This is the PIPEDA-accountability paper trail.\n\n---\n\n## 6. The build checklist — what's done, what to add\n\n**Already in place (the gate is doing legal work):**\n\n- ✅ Opt-out (reply STOP) — this is both the CASL unsubscribe **and** the CRTC internal do-not-call list.\n- ✅ Quiet hours — the mechanism that keeps sends inside legal calling hours.\n- ✅ Daily caps + approval-before-send — reduce the blast-radius of a mistake.\n- ✅ The 10% estimate ceiling as a database rule.\n- ✅ Customer-facing privacy policy and data-deletion pages.\n\n**To add or confirm before going paid:**\n\n1. **Sender identification in every templated message** — business name + contact. Audit each machine's copy for it.\n2. **Consent-window enforcement on reactivation/upsell** — the gate should refuse a send whose only basis is a transaction older than 2 years (or an inquiry older than 6 months) with no express opt-in on file. This is a new gate check worth building.\n3. **Opt-out retention = 3 years + 14 days** — confirm the opt-out records aren't purged before then.\n4. **Quiet-hours outer bound** — clamp any client's quiet-hours setting so sends can never fall outside 9:00–21:30 weekday / 10:00–18:00 weekend local.\n5. **Register the operating business with the National DNCL** — required even though most sends are exempt.\n6. **Voice greeting discloses AI + recording** — one line at call start.\n7. **Outbound AI voice refused by default** — make it a capability that can't be switched on without an express-consent gate.\n8. **Photo scrub-on-intake** — resize and strip metadata before storage; enforce the no-faces/plates/addresses rule in the intake step, not just in instructions.\n9. **Confirm and disclose data-storage region.**\n10. **Consent record-keeping** — store *when and how* consent was obtained per contact, so an audit can be answered.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Watch list (laws mid-change — check before they land)\n\n- **CRTC 2026-132** — a live consultation, open for comment, reviewing the Unsolicited Telecommunications Rules and **aligning them more closely with CASL.** Could change consent definitions and how texts are treated. Worth tracking; nothing to do today.\n- **Ontario CPA, 2023** — passed, not in force, regulations pending. Will add express-consent-to-amend, contract-disclosure, and cancellation rules. Design the customer agreement to absorb it later.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Questions this raises for the solicitor\n\nThese slot straight into the existing solicitor brief:\n\n1. Client service agreement + acceptable-use + the no-risk guarantee wording — does the liability split hold up?\n2. Data-processing addendum — is our \"service provider\" framing right, and does the privacy policy's cross-border/storage-region line satisfy PIPEDA?\n3. The estimate/quote template under CPA 2002 today, and whether to pre-wire it for CPA 2023.\n4. Photo retention — must the **original** be kept, or is a scrubbed/resized copy sufficient and preferable? (This decides originals vs. 1568-px copies in storage.)\n5. Is texting-back a missed call ever a CEM in his view, or always a service message — i.e., how careful must missed-call and speed-to-lead copy be?\n6. Confirm: outbound AI voice needs express consent + DNCL + ADAD compliance before we ever enable it.\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n- [CRTC — Key Unsolicited Telecommunications Rules](https://crtc.gc.ca/eng/phone/telemarketing/tobligations/rules-regles.htm)\n- [CRTC — CASL Guidance on Implied Consent](https://crtc.gc.ca/eng/com500/guide.htm)\n- [CRTC — Notice of Consultation 2026-132 (UTR review / CASL alignment)](https://crtc.gc.ca/eng/archive/2026/2026-132.htm)\n- [Office of the Privacy Commissioner — PIPEDA and the Consent principle](https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/p_principle/principles/p_consent/)\n- [Osler — Ontario Consumer Protection Act, 2023: regulations taking shape (status)](https://www.osler.com/en/insights/updates/incoming-consumer-protection-changes-ontario-regulations-begin-taking-shape/)\n- [BLG — Ontario's new Consumer Protection Act: proposed measures](https://www.blg.com/en/insights/2025/01/ontarios-new-consumer-protection-act-proposed-measures)\n\n*Not legal advice. Confirm with the Ontario solicitor before the first paid Category B install.*\n"},{"date":"2026-09-14","group":"life","kind":"guide","label":"Rebuild notes","origin":"original","source":"project doc claude/five-core-rebuild-2026-09-14.md","title":"5 Core rebuild — four groups, work log, Arabic course (shipped Sep 14–15 2026)","md":"# 5 Core rebuild — four groups, work log, Arabic course (shipped Sep 14–15 2026)\n\nLive: https://blondedca-wq.github.io/5-core-systems/index.html · Repo `blondedca-wq/5-core-systems` · commit `86523d4` on `main`.\nVerified live: index.html sha256 `a328d243…`, journal.js `84b9bbb4…`, arabic.js `b9c42c21…`, CONTEXT.md `7d877de9…` — byte-identical to the build; rendered in Ahmed's Chrome with his real localStorage (Quran reads, roadmap ticks, bills, habits, workouts all intact).\n\n## What Ahmed asked for → what shipped\n\n| Ask | Shipped |\n|---|---|\n| Four groups (Work · Life · Fitness · Study) in one day view | Shared `renderDayGroups()` used by Today and the calendar day sheet. Every item routes through `CAT_GROUP` → group. |\n| Work/study summaries on each calendar day, all history labelled, keep up to date | `journal.js` → `window.JOURNAL` (dated entries Jul 2 → today: Lab kickoff, sprint days, A·Day 1–17, B·Day 1–2, get-ahead day) + `window.SPRINT_PLAN` (67 dated roadmap days A1…G3 with checkbox keys = the roadmap's own `lab.roadmap.done.v1` keys). Calendar chips: `A·D17 The contract day…`, `Arabic D3`, `📖 Kafirun`, `🛒 Groceries + meal prep`, `🥣 Stock-up + lifts`. Quran read dates derive from `planner.quran.read.v1` timestamps; Arabic lesson dates from `planner.arabic.v1`. Work tab = stats hero, up-next roadmap card, log by month, planned days. |\n| Life = bills/finances, summary, misc events; income $4k; groceries + meal prep Monday | `INCOME = 4000`; Monday 8:30–10:30 PM \"Grocery shop + meal prep + weekly review\" (Sat/Sun items removed); Summary tab gains a \"Month log\" card. |\n| Fitness = workout + grocery list; monthly oatmeal + pre + protein, +$150 | Grocery tab: Weekly $65–85 · Monthly stock-up $150 (first Monday) · Monthly total $410–490. Stock-up checkboxes keyed per month (`planner.groceries.monthly.v1`). |\n| Study = Quran + Arabic for a complete beginner, Day 1/Day 2 (no dates), phonetics for memorizing, weave together, goal Quranic fusha | `arabic.js` → 60-day course in 4 phases (Read the script 1–12 · Words of the Quran 13–30 · Grammar 31–45 · Speak fusha 46–60). Each day: learn blocks, a real Quran passage (Arabic + phonetic + meaning, fetched live), memorize target, speak lines, self-check. Memorization path Fatihah → 112–114 → … → Ayat al-Kursi (Days 20–24) → … → 67 (Days 54–58). Reader gets a phonetic toggle (`planner.phonetic.v1`). Progress in `planner.arabic.v1`, cloud-synced like everything else. |\n| Keep all progress | No localStorage key renamed or dropped. |\n\n## Keep-it-up-to-date protocol (also in the repo's CONTEXT.md)\n\nAt the close of every work or study session:\n1. Append ONE entry to `window.JOURNAL` in `journal.js`: `{ date:'YYYY-MM-DD', group:'work'|'study'|'life'|'fitness', tag:'B · Day 3', title, bullets:[…], sources:[…] }`. Never edit history; add. Sunday-night sessions are dated the Sunday.\n2. Write that day's study guide and append it to `window.GUIDES` in `guides.js` (same date + group as the journal entry). Originals go in as written; if a guide has to be written after the fact, mark it `reconstructed`.\n3. If `roadmap.html` BLOCKS changed, regenerate `window.SPRINT_PLAN` (parse BLOCKS; `Day N · Ddd Mon D` → date; `keys` = `'b'+block.id+'-'+flatIndex` in step order).\n4. Bump `?v=` on the `<script src=\"journal.js?v=…\">`, `guides.js?v=…` and `arabic.js?v=…` tags in `index.html`.\n5. Quran/Arabic dates need nothing — derived from synced progress timestamps.\nShip to `main`, verify live (hard reload / `?v=`).\n\n## How it was deployed (no git push from the sandbox)\n\nThe sandbox proxy refuses `git push` and the GitHub API; Chrome's `file_upload` refuses paths outside the session. What worked: build locally → `manifest.json` = `{file: {b64: base64(gzip -9), sha, size}}` → split into 20,000-char chunks → open `github.com/<repo>/upload/main` in Ahmed's Chrome → append each chunk to `window.__m` via `javascript_tool` (template literal; verify each chunk's SHA-256 in-page with `crypto.subtle` against the local hash) → `DecompressionStream('gzip')` (writer/reader) → per-file SHA-256 check → `File` objects → `DataTransfer` → `input.files` + `change` event → GitHub uploads, fill title, click \"Commit changes\". Then `git fetch` in the clone to compare blob hashes, and curl the Pages URLs with a cache-buster.\n\n## Sep 15 2026 — all-time PRs + lifts sync (commit `b475d39`)\n\nAhmed: \"you dont have my prs from all time go into data and have them on all exercises\". His PRs live in the 5 Core workout log **on his phone** (`planner.workoutLog.v2` was local-only — never in the ntfy payload — so desktop/cloud had nothing).\n\nShipped:\n- **All-time PR board** at the top of Fitness: every exercise in the split + every retired exercise ever logged, with PR (heaviest weight, ties → more reps; bodyweight moves → most reps), date, sessions, e1RM (Epley).\n- **PR on every exercise row**, in the set logger (\"🏆 PR 205 × 6 (date) — beat it\"), on the Today / day-sheet Fitness card (\"Day N — PRs to beat\" for the next split day, plus 🏆 on logged entries that were a PR), new-PR confetti + toast on save.\n- **History folded across split versions** via `W_ALIASES` (e.g. `triceps-rope-pushdown` → `rope-pushdown`, `incline-db-bench-light-` → `incline-dumbbell-press`, `rear-delt-fly` → `cable-rear-delt-fly`, plus common shorthand). Different movements stay separate and show as \"Not in current split\".\n- **Lifts cloud sync** on its own ntfy topic: snapshot `{log, split, cycle}` → gzip → base64 → chunked ≤3,000 chars per message (ntfy turns any message >4,096 B into a 3-hour attachment, which would silently break sync — the MAIN topic payload is ~2.5 KB and must stay <4 KB too). Reader reassembles the newest complete set; log merges as a union keyed by date|weight|reps|sets; split/cycle newest `_m` wins; polls every 15 s; publishes on save and (rate-limited) when the cloud lacks what the device holds.\n\nState at close: desktop board shows the empty state until Ahmed opens 5 Core on his phone once (phone pushes, desktop pulls within a minute).\n\n## Sep 15 2026 — study guides on every Work day\n\nAhmed: \"these days i completed in the work section dont include all study guides to each day corrilated why?\" — because the log only ever held bullet summaries; the guides lived as separate files in the SecondRing Vault / project docs, and only a few days had one. Fixed: `guides.js` → `window.GUIDES`, one or more guides per journal day. Originals embedded as written (Day 1–2 session log + cheat sheet + Build 1 page, Day 16 findings + startup prompt, Day 17 study guide + findings + startup prompt + schema notes, B Day 1 lawful-business guide, B Day 2 status + scope contract); every other Work day got a reconstructed plain-English guide (what the day was · what changed and the sales line · glossary · how to check it yourself · lessons · sources), labelled *reconstructed*. Read from the Work tab (📘 chips under each day) and from the day sheet; the reader has prev/next-day navigation.\n"},{"date":"2026-09-15","group":"work","kind":"status","label":"Where it stands","origin":"original","source":"SecondRing Vault/Day 18/b2-day2-status.md","title":"Category B Day 2 — Photo → Quote AI I : where it stands","md":"# Category B Day 2 — Photo → Quote AI I : where it stands\n\n*Tue Sep 15. Built and proven against the live database and the live storage layer, except the one box that needs real photos.*\n\n## The four boxes\n\n**1. Photo intake → Supabase Storage → signed URLs — DONE.**\nThe `job-photos` bucket, its tenant-scoped storage RLS (`storage_tenant_of(name)`, path `<tenant_id>/…`), and the generic `files` registry were already in place. Added `sr_photo_intake(tenant, path, subject_type, subject_id, …)` — it registers an uploaded photo against a job or contact, enforces the tenant path prefix, and logs a `photo.intake` event. Signed URLs are minted from the Storage API (`/storage/v1/object/sign/job-photos/<path>`), which the extraction workflow does per photo.\n\n**2. Vision model extracts scope — BUILT, needs one real photo to validate.**\nThe contract is fixed in `b2-scope-contract.md`: the exact prompt, the JSON schema (`item_type, category_hint, quantity, dimensions{}, materials[], condition, access, hazards[], measurements_visible, confidence, needs_more_photos, missing_info[], quote_hints{}`), and the model (Haiku 4.5, ~$0.0016/photo). The workflow `scope-extract-workflow.json` is built webhook-style like the onboarding one — `POST /webhook/scope-extract {tenant_id, file_id, path, job_id?}` → sign URL → Claude vision (Anthropic credential) → parse JSON → `sr_save_scope_draft` → respond. **Two things a real photo will confirm:** the exact Haiku 4.5 model id (currently `claude-haiku-4-5` — may need the dated suffix on the live account) and that the image-by-URL source works. Both surface on the first photo, which box 4 provides.\n\n**3. Scope draft saved as structured data against the contact and job — DONE.**\nNew `scope_drafts` table (RLS on): the structured scope, `item_type`, model, confidence, and a `usable` flag, linked to `file_id`, `contact_id`, and `job_id`. `sr_save_scope_draft(…)` writes it, derives the job/contact from the photo's subject when not passed, stamps the file as analysed, and logs a `scope.extracted` event with the guardrail result. `sr_scope_read(tenant, job_id)` reads a job's drafts back for Day 3's quote step.\n\n**4. Ten seeded photo sets + measure usable rate — NEEDS YOUR PHOTOS.**\nThe measurement is a database rule, not a guess: `sr_scope_usable(scope, confidence)` returns true only when the scope names the item, carries a measurement or count, describes condition and access, isn't flagged `needs_more_photos`, and confidence ≥ 0.55. `sr_scope_stats(tenant, since)` reports `total / usable / usable_pct / avg_confidence / by_item`. Proven end-to-end on synthetic data — a full water-heater scope passed, a bare \"pipe\" failed despite 0.9 confidence, giving 50% usable exactly as designed.\n\n## To finish Day 2 (needs you)\n\nDrop **10 photo sets from real jobs** into the vault (no faces, plates, addresses, or paperwork in frame), including one deliberately bad set. Then, in one pass: I import and publish `scope-extract-workflow.json`, set its secret, and POST each photo through — validating the Haiku model id and the vision output on the first one — then `sr_scope_stats` prints the real usable rate. That's box 4 and it closes Day 2.\n\nAlso owed for Day 5: **one PII-scrubbed blueprint** with a known final cost.\n\n## Files\n- `b2-scope-contract.md` — the vision prompt + JSON schema + usable rubric (project doc + vault).\n- `b2-vision-pipeline.sql` — the applied DDL (scope_drafts + 5 functions).\n- `scope-extract-workflow.json` — the n8n workflow, ready to import.\n"},{"date":"2026-09-15","group":"work","kind":"contract","label":"Scope contract","origin":"original","source":"SecondRing Vault/Day 18/b2-scope-contract.md","title":"Scope-extraction contract — Photo → Quote AI I","md":"# Scope-extraction contract — Photo → Quote AI I\n\n*Category B Day 2. The contract between a job photo and a structured scope draft. The vision model reads a photo and must return **exactly** the JSON below; the pipeline saves it with `sr_save_scope_draft`, and `sr_scope_usable` (a database function, not a habit) decides whether it's good enough to quote from.*\n\n---\n\n## The flow, in one line\n\nPhone → `job-photos/<tenant_id>/<job_id>/<uuid>.jpg` → `sr_photo_intake` registers the file → signed URL → **Claude vision + this prompt** → the JSON below → `sr_save_scope_draft` writes it against the contact and job → `sr_scope_usable` flags it usable or not.\n\n## Model and cost\n\nStart on **Claude Haiku 4.5** — ~$0.0016 a photo, the whole ten-set run is under $1. Escalate a photo to Sonnet only if Haiku returns `needs_more_photos` or confidence below the floor and the photo is clearly good. Resize the long edge to **1568 px before upload** — that's the cap the model applies to a standard-tier image anyway, so it costs nothing in quality and turns 1 GB of storage from ~40 quotes into ~300.\n\n---\n\n## The output schema (the model must return only this)\n\n```json\n{\n  \"item_type\": \"gas water heater\",\n  \"category_hint\": \"water_heater_replace\",\n  \"quantity\": 1,\n  \"dimensions\": { \"capacity_gal\": 40, \"height_in\": 58, \"notes\": \"label partly obscured\" },\n  \"materials\": [\"copper flex connectors\", \"B-vent\", \"T&P valve\"],\n  \"condition\": \"8–12 years old, corrosion at the base, minor rust on fittings\",\n  \"access\": \"basement utility closet, tight, down a flight of stairs — two-person carry\",\n  \"hazards\": [\"gas line present\", \"low overhead clearance\"],\n  \"measurements_visible\": true,\n  \"confidence\": 0.72,\n  \"needs_more_photos\": false,\n  \"missing_info\": [\"exact BTU rating not readable\"],\n  \"quote_hints\": { \"likely_line_items\": [\"water_heater_replace_40gal\"], \"emergency\": false }\n}\n```\n\n### Field rules\n\n- **item_type** *(required)* — plain words for what the job is about. \"gas water heater\", \"asphalt shingle roof section\", \"kitchen sink P-trap\". Not a sentence.\n- **category_hint** — a slug the quote step can match toward `rate_card_items.code`/`aliases`. Best guess; the pricing engine does the real matching.\n- **quantity** — a number when countable (3 pot lights, 12 m of baseboard). Omit or 1 for a single item.\n- **dimensions** — an object of measurable facts with units in the key (`length_ft`, `capacity_gal`, `area_sqft`, `diameter_in`). Only what's actually visible or reliably inferable from a reference object. Empty object if nothing measurable.\n- **materials** — what it's made of / what it connects to, as an array. Drives materials lines.\n- **condition** *(required)* — age, wear, damage, corrosion, code issues. This is what separates a repair from a replacement.\n- **access** *(required)* — how a tech physically reaches and works the item. Tight, stairs, height, crawlspace, one-person vs two. This is the single most under-quoted labour driver, so it's mandatory.\n- **hazards** — gas, live electrical, asbestos-era material, water, confined space. Safety and pricing both.\n- **measurements_visible** — true only if a real dimension could be read or scaled from the photo.\n- **confidence** *(required, 0–1)* — the model's own honest read on whether this scope is quote-ready. Low is fine and useful; a confident wrong scope is the expensive failure.\n- **needs_more_photos** — true when one angle isn't enough (e.g. can't see the connection, the whole run, or the far side). Setting this true forces \"not usable\" no matter what else is filled.\n- **missing_info** — the specific facts a human would still need to ask for.\n- **quote_hints** — optional leg-up for Day 3: candidate line items and whether it reads as an emergency.\n\n---\n\n## The vision prompt (verbatim)\n\n> You are a scoping assistant for a skilled-trades business (plumbing, HVAC, electrical, roofing, general repair). You are looking at ONE photo a customer or technician took of a job. Your job is to extract a structured **scope** a tradesperson could quote from — not to write a quote, and not to invent a price.\n>\n> Rules:\n> - Describe only what you can actually see or reliably infer from a reference object in the frame. Do not guess a measurement you cannot ground.\n> - If one photo isn't enough to scope the job, say so by setting `needs_more_photos` to true and listing what's missing.\n> - Condition and access matter as much as the item itself — a tradesperson prices the difficulty, not just the part. Always fill `condition` and `access`.\n> - Be honest in `confidence`. A low score on a bad photo is correct and useful. A confident wrong scope costs the business money.\n> - Note any hazard you see (gas, live electrical, water, height, confined space, asbestos-era material).\n> - Do not identify people, read faces, or transcribe any personal or account information visible in the frame. Scope the work, not the people.\n>\n> Return **only** a JSON object with these keys: `item_type`, `category_hint`, `quantity`, `dimensions` (object), `materials` (array), `condition`, `access`, `hazards` (array), `measurements_visible` (boolean), `confidence` (0–1), `needs_more_photos` (boolean), `missing_info` (array), `quote_hints` (object). No prose before or after the JSON.\n\n*(When several photos belong to one job, run each through this prompt, save each as its own draft, then let Day 3 aggregate the drafts into one quote — a single bad angle then can't sink a good job.)*\n\n---\n\n## The \"usable\" rubric — a database rule\n\n`sr_scope_usable(scope jsonb, confidence numeric)` returns true only when **all** hold:\n\n1. `item_type` is present and non-empty — *what it is*.\n2. There's a **measurable**: at least one key in `dimensions`, **or** a `quantity` greater than zero.\n3. `condition` is present and non-empty.\n4. `access` is present and non-empty.\n5. `needs_more_photos` is not true.\n6. `confidence` ≥ **0.55**.\n\nThis is the yardstick box 4 measures against — \"how often is scope extraction usable\" is just `usable / total` from `sr_scope_stats`. It lives in one function so the number can't drift with a workflow edit.\n\n**Privacy note (ties to the lawful-business guide):** the prompt is told not to identify people or read personal information, and intake resizes to 1568 px. Whether the *original* photo must be retained or a scrubbed copy is enough is solicitor question 4 — until answered, keep the resized copy and treat originals as delete-on-quote.\n\n---\n\n## What still needs Ahmed\n\nThe pipeline is built and the contract is fixed. Box 4 — running ten real photo sets and measuring the usable rate — needs the photos: **10 sets from real jobs** (no faces, plates, addresses, or paperwork in frame), including one deliberately bad set so the \"not usable\" path is exercised, plus **one PII-scrubbed blueprint** with a known final cost for Day 5. Drop them in and the harness runs the whole set through and prints the usable rate in one go.\n"}];
